window.KB_BODY[59]={"tsb101258":"<h2>Revision to Procedures 008-018 and 008-019</h2><h3>Warranty Statement</h3><p>The information in this document has no effect on present warranty coverage or repair practices, nor does it authorize TRP or Campaign actions.</p><h3>Contents</h3><p>This service/parts topic updates Procedure 008-018, Radiator Cap Pressure Release Test, and Procedure 008-019, Air or Combustion Gas in Cooling System, in the Signature, ISX, and QSX15 Base Engine Troubleshooting and Repair Manual, Bulletin <a class=\"lnk man\" href=\"#/manual/3666239\">3666239</a>.</p>","tsb101262":"<h2>Revision to Procedure 002-016, Valve Guide Seal, Cylinder Head</h2><h3>Warranty Statement</h3><p>The information in this document has no effect on present warranty coverage or repair practices, nor does it authorize TRP or Campaign actions.</p><h3>Contents</h3><p>This service/parts topic updates Procedure 002-016, Valve Guide Seal, Cylinder Head in the Signature, ISX, and QSX15 Troubleshooting and Repair Manual, Bulletin <a class=\"lnk man\" href=\"#/manual/3666239\">3666239</a>.</p>","tsb101264":"<h2>Revision to Procedure 002-004, Cylinder Head</h2><h3>Warranty Statement</h3><p>The information in this document has no effect on present warranty coverage or repair practices, nor does it authorize TRP or Campaign actions.</p><h3>Contents</h3><p>This service/parts topic updates Procedure 002-004, Cylinder Head, for the Signature, ISX, and QSX15 Base Engine Troubleshooting and Repair Manual, Bulletin <a class=\"lnk man\" href=\"#/manual/3666239\">3666239</a>.</p>","tsb101265":"<h2>Revision to Procedure 008-047, Radiator Pressure Cap</h2><h3>Warranty Statement</h3><p>The information in this document has no effect on present warranty coverage or repair practices, nor does it authorize TRP or Campaign actions.</p><h3>Contents</h3><p>This service/parts topic revises Procedure 008-047 in the Signature, ISX, and QSX15 Base Engine Troubleshooting and Repair Manual, Bulletin <a class=\"lnk man\" href=\"#/manual/3666239\">3666239</a>.</p>","tsb101277":"<h2>New Oversize Head Gasket</h2><h3>Warranty Statement</h3><p>The information in this document has no effect on present warranty coverage or repair practices, nor does it authorize TRP or Campaign actions.</p><h3>Contents</h3><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/02600044.png\" alt=\"02600044.png\"></figure><p>This service/parts topic introduces a new oversize head gasket and wear limits for the cylinder block head deck of the QSK45 and QSK60 engines. The new cylinder head gasket, Part Number 4086730, has been released to allow the repair of worn cylinder blocks at engine rebuild/overhaul. The new gasket makes use of the same material and form as the existing gasket but is 0.51 mm [0.020 in] thicker. The gasket has a notch in the carrier to denote the gasket is a service oversize gasket, see the illustration above.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/02600045.png\" alt=\"02600045.png\"></figure><p>The illustration above shows the dimensions for the oversize gasket.</p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>mm</th><th></th><th>in</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>3.12</td><td>MIN</td><td>0.123</td></tr><tr><td>3.48</td><td>MAX</td><td>0.137</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>mm</th><th></th><th>in</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>2.43</td><td>MIN</td><td>0.096</td></tr><tr><td>2.59</td><td>MAX</td><td>0.102</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Cylinder block head decks wear due to several factors, some are listed below:</p><p>Dirt under the gasket</p><ul><li>In operation, fine particles of dirt can get under the head gasket where it becomes trapped. In operation, the cylinder head moves on the head gasket (very small amounts) and the gasket moves on the cylinder block. When dirt is trapped between the gasket and the block, motion frets the cylinder block surface.</li></ul><p>Coolant leaks</p><ul><li>If coolant leaks from the head gasket, the counterbore, or leaks externally, corrosion can start. The cylinder head deck is eroded by corrosion and pitting.</li></ul>","tsb101278":"<h2>Procedure 001-089, Block Stiffener Plate</h2><h3>Warranty Statement</h3><p>The information in this document has no effect on present warranty coverage or repair practices, nor does it authorize TRP or Campaign actions.</p><h3>Contents</h3><p>This service/parts topic introduces the block stiffener plate for M11, ISM, and QSM11 series engines and assists repair and service personnel in removal and installation procedures.</p><p>The 2002 ISM engine with EGR features a block stiffener to add ridigity to the block. Older non-EGR M11/ISM engines can be upfitted with the block stiffener plate, but will require additional parts.</p><div class=\"callout note\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">i</span>Note · Примечание</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>This is a product improvement and is <b>not</b> subject to campaign.</p></div></div><p>This topic includes the following:</p><ol><li>ISM with exhaust gas recirculation (EGR), engine serial number (ESN) first and build date</li><li>Parts List for EGR and Non-EGR Engines</li><li>ISM/M11 Non-EGR installation procedures</li><li>Block stiffener plate remove and install procedures.</li></ol><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>ISM with EGR, ESN First and Build Date</th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>35067942</td><td>August 23, 2002</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Parts List for EGR and Non-EGR Engines</th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Part Description</td><td>Part Number</td><td>Quantity</td></tr><tr><td>Block stiffener plate</td><td>4082891</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>Stamped steel oil pan capscrew</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/3896724\">3896724</a></td><td>32</td></tr><tr><td>Aluminum oil pan capscrew</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/3089016\">3089016</a></td><td>32</td></tr><tr><td>Torx head capscrew</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/4082772\">4082772</a></td><td>18</td></tr><tr><td>oil pan gasket</td><td>3401290</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>Loctite 5999</td><td>3164070</td><td>As required</td></tr><tr><td>Rear sump oil pan lube transfer tube</td><td>4082897</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>Middle sump oil pan lube transfer tube</td><td>4083029</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>Oil suction tube o-rings</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/3032787\">3032787</a></td><td>As required</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Loctite 5999 sealant comes in a tube that can be adequate for up to four block stiffener plate installations. Substitute 3 Bond RTV (gray or red) if Loctite 5999 is unavailable.</p><p>Non-EGR engines require additional parts for block stiffener plate initial installation. All oil pans require longer capscrews. Middle and rear sump oil pans require redesigned oil transfer tubes.</p>","tsb101282":"<h2>Main Bearing Inspect for Reuse Guidelines and Repair</h2><h3>Warranty Statement</h3><p>The information in this document has no effect on present warranty coverage or repair practices, nor does it authorize TRP or Campaign actions.</p><h3>Contents</h3><p>This service/parts topic obsoletes 00T1-4A. Replace 00T1-4A with this topic.</p><p>This service/parts topic provides information to assist all repair and service personnel in diagnosing main cap fretting on M11 and ISM series engines. This topic includes the following:</p><ul><li>Main cap fretting general information</li><li>Main bearing and bore inspect for reuse guidelines</li><li>Main bearing Plastigage® procedures</li><li>Main bearing condemnation limits</li><li>Loctite® 518 procedures</li><li>Block repair options.</li></ul><p>Main bearing capscrew torque procedures:</p><ul><li>Torque-Plus-Angle method</li><li>Torque-To-Yield method.</li></ul><p>The following kit part numbers may be used to repair blocks.</p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Part Description</th><th>Part Number</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Service block kit</td><td>4070793NX</td></tr><tr><td>ReCon® service block kit</td><td>4070793RX</td></tr></tbody></table></div>","tsb101286":"<h2>Revision to Procedure 013-020, Starting Motor</h2><h3>Warranty Statement</h3><p>The information in this document has no effect on present warranty coverage or repair practices, nor does it authorize TRP or Campaign actions.</p><h3>Contents</h3><p>This Service/Parts Topic revises Procedure <a class=\"lnk doc\" href=\"#/doc/10-013-020\">013-020</a>, Starting Motor for the Signature, ISX, and QSX15 Base Engine Troubleshooting and Repair Manual, Bulletin <a class=\"lnk man\" href=\"#/manual/3666239\">3666239</a>.</p>","tsb101295":"<h2>Fault Code 254 Troubleshooting Tree Update</h2><h3>Warranty Statement</h3><p>The information in this document has no effect on present warranty coverage or repair practices, nor does it authorize TRP or Campaign actions.</p><h3>Contents</h3><p>This service/parts topic updates Fault Code 254 - Fuel Shutoff Valve Solenoid Supply Circuit in the Troubleshooting and Repair Manual, Signature, ISX and QSX15 Engines, Bulletin 3666259.</p>","tsb101296":"<h2>Fan Hub Bearing Spacer</h2><h3>Warranty Statement</h3><p>The information in this document has no effect on present warranty coverage or repair practices, nor does it authorize TRP or Campaign actions.</p><h3>Contents</h3><p>This service/parts topic introduces a new fan hub bearing spacer for the QSK45 and QSK60 engines equipped with a direct drive fan hub. The spacer is located between the two bearings in the fan hub. Its purpose is to maintain preload on the bearings within the fan hub to increase durability.</p><p>Previous to the introduction of this spacer, the fan hub for QSK45 and QSK60 engines did <b>not</b> contain a spacer. When replacing the bearings or rebuilding the fan hub on engines built prior to the introduction of the fan hub bearing spacer, install the new spacer to the fan hub.</p><p>The spacer is denoted by arrow 12 in Figure 1 below.</p><p>The new spacer, Part Number 4100760, was introduced in production at Daventry with engine serial number first 33152994 on 19-August-2002 and is compatible with all QSK45 and QSK60 engines fitted with the direct drive fan hub.</p><p>This service/parts topic also includes a revision of procedure 008-036 (Fan Hub, Belt Driven) to include the installation of the fan hub bearing spacer.</p><p>This service/parts topic supercedes 02T8-4; replace 02T8-4 with this topic.</p><div class=\"callout note\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">i</span>Note · Примечание</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>This is a product improvement <b>not</b> subject to campaign.</p></div></div><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/fa600ab.png\" alt=\"fa600ab.png\"></figure><p>Figure 1</p>","tsb101297":"<h2>Update to Procedure 005-043 (Fuel Shutoff Valve)</h2><h3>Warranty Statement</h3><p>The information in this document has no effect on present warranty coverage or repair practices, nor does it authorize TRP or Campaign actions.</p><h3>Contents</h3><p>This service/parts topic is to update procedure <a class=\"lnk doc\" href=\"#/doc/10-005-043\">005-043</a> in Troubleshooting and Repair Manual, Signature, ISX and QSX15 Engines, Bulletin <a class=\"lnk man\" href=\"#/manual/3666239\">3666239</a>.</p>","tsb101298":"<h2>Update to Procedure 001-026, Cylinder Block</h2><h3>Warranty Statement</h3><p>The information in this document has no effect on present warranty coverage or repair practices, nor does it authorize TRP or Campaign actions.</p><h3>Contents</h3><p>This service/parts topic updates Procedure 001-026, Cylinder Block, in Troubleshooting and Repair Manual, Signature, ISX and QSX15 Engines, Bulletin <a class=\"lnk man\" href=\"#/manual/3666239\">3666239</a>.</p><p>A repair step has been added to repair minor defects in the cylinder block using a taper-threaded plug.</p>","tsb101300":"<h2>Update to Procedure 001-043</h2><h3>Warranty Statement</h3><p>The information in this document has no effect on present warranty coverage or repair practices, nor does it authorize TRP or Campaign actions.</p><h3>Contents</h3><p>This service/parts topic updates procedure <a class=\"lnk doc\" href=\"#/doc/10-001-043\">001-043</a> in Troubleshooting and Repair Manual, Signature, ISX and QSX15 Engines, Bulletin <a class=\"lnk man\" href=\"#/manual/3666239\">3666239</a>.</p>","tsb101301":"<h2>Revision to Procedure 010-050, Turbocharger Wastegate Actuator</h2><h3>Warranty Statement</h3><p>The information in this document has no effect on present warranty coverage or repair practices, nor does it authorize TRP or Campaign actions.</p><h3>Contents</h3><p>This service/parts topic revises Procedure 010-050, Turbocharger Wastegate Actuator for the Signature, ISX, and QSX15 Base Engine Troubleshooting and Repair Manual, Bulletin <a class=\"lnk man\" href=\"#/manual/3666239\">3666239</a>.</p>","tsb101302":"<h2>Procedure 011-009, Exhaust Restriction</h2><h3>Warranty Statement</h3><p>The information in this document has no effect on present warranty coverage or repair practices, nor does it authorize TRP or Campaign actions.</p><h3>Contents</h3><p>This service/parts topic revises Procedure 011-009, Exhaust Restriction to include Marine application information for the C Series Troubleshooting and Repair Manual, Bulletin <a class=\"lnk man\" href=\"#/manual/3666003\">3666003</a>.</p>","tsb101303":"<h2>Procedure 001-089, Block Stiffener Plate</h2><h3>Warranty Statement</h3><p>The information in this document has no effect on present warranty coverage or repair practices, nor does it authorize TRP or Campaign actions.</p><h3>Contents</h3><p>This service/parts topic introduces the block stiffener plate for C8.3G, C Gas Plus, 6C, and C8.3 series engines. This topic also assists repair and service personnel in removal and installation procedures.</p><div class=\"callout note\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">i</span>Note · Примечание</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>This is a product improvement and is <b>not</b> subject to campaign.</p></div></div>","tsb101306":"<h2>Intake Air Heater System Troubleshooting Procedures</h2><h3>Warranty Statement</h3><p>The information in this document has no effect on present warranty coverage or repair practices, nor does it authorize TRP or Campaign actions.</p><h3>Contents</h3><p>This Service/Parts Topic updates the following for the C Series Engines Troubleshooting and Repair Manual, Bulletin <a class=\"lnk man\" href=\"#/manual/3666003\">3666003</a>:</p><ul><li>Intake Manifold Air Heater System Malfunctioning symptom tree</li><li>Procedure <a class=\"lnk doc\" href=\"#/doc/41-010-999\">010-999</a>, Air Intake System - Overview</li><li>Procedure <a class=\"lnk doc\" href=\"#/doc/100-010-122\">010-122</a>, Intake Manifold Air Heater Wiring Harness</li><li>Procedure <a class=\"lnk doc\" href=\"#/doc/100-010-123\">010-123</a>, Intake Manifold Air Heater Temperature Sensor</li><li>Procedure <a class=\"lnk doc\" href=\"#/doc/100-010-124\">010-124</a>, Intake Manifold Air Heater Element</li><li>Procedure <a class=\"lnk doc\" href=\"#/doc/100-010-125\">010-125</a>, Intake Manifold Air Heater Control Module</li><li>Procedure <a class=\"lnk doc\" href=\"#/doc/100-010-126\">010-126</a>, Intake Manifold Air Heater Solenoid Switch</li><li>Procedure <a class=\"lnk doc\" href=\"#/doc/100-010-127\">010-127</a>, Intake Manifold Air Heater Speed Sensor.</li></ul>","tsb101308":"<h2>Revised Oil Pan Capscrew Torque Sequence and Torque Value</h2><h3>Warranty Statement</h3><p>The information in this document has no effect on present warranty coverage or repair practices, nor does it authorize TRP or Campaign actions.</p><h3>Contents</h3><p>This service/parts topic revises the torquing sequence and torque values of the oil pan capscrews.</p>","tsb101309":"<h2>Revised Service Tools List</h2><h3>Warranty Statement</h3><p>The information in this document has no effect on present warranty coverage or repair practices, nor does it authorize TRP or Campaign actions.</p><h3>Contents</h3><p>This service/parts topic is adding the Filter Head Adapter Replacer, Part Number 3165082 and Sealing/Retainer Compound, Part Number 3165083 to the Service Tools List.</p>","tsb101310":"<h2>Revised Lubricating Oil Filter Head Adapter Torquing Sequence</h2><h3>Warranty Statement</h3><p>The information in this document has no effect on present warranty coverage or repair practices, nor does it authorize TRP or Campaign actions.</p><h3>Contents</h3><p>This service/parts topic is adding a service tool and updating procedure 007-018 for the removal and installation of the lubricating oil filter head adapter.</p>","tsb101314":"<h2>Revised Fuel System Procedures, 005-012 and 005-999</h2><h3>Warranty Statement</h3><p>The information in this document has no effect on present warranty coverage or repair practices, nor does it authorize TRP or Campaign actions.</p><h3>Contents</h3><p>This service/parts topic revises Procedures, 005-012 and 005-999 for the C6 and C8.3 engine series.</p>","tsb101315":"<h2>Revision to Troubleshooting Trees</h2><h3>Warranty Statement</h3><p>The information in this document has no effect on present warranty coverage or repair practices, nor does it authorize TRP or Campaign actions.</p><h3>Contents</h3><p>This Service/Parts Topic revises troubleshooting trees for C Series engines.</p>","tsb101317":"<h2>Revision to Procedure 007-025, Lubricating Oil Pan</h2><h3>Warranty Statement</h3><p>The information in this document has no effect on present warranty coverage or repair practices, nor does it authorize TRP or Campaign actions.</p><h3>Contents</h3><p>This service/parts topic revises Procedure 007-025, Lubricating Oil Pan for:</p><ul><li>C Series Engines Troubleshooting and Repair Manual, Bulletin <a class=\"lnk man\" href=\"#/manual/3666003\">3666003</a></li><li>C8.3 (Natural Gas) Base Engine Troubleshooting and Repair Manual, Bulletin 3666206.</li></ul>","tsb101318":"<h2>Procedure 011-028, Cylinder Liner</h2><h3>Warranty Statement</h3><p>The information in this document has no effect on present warranty coverage or repair practices, nor does it authorize TRP or Campaign actions.</p><h3>Contents</h3><p>This service/parts topic revises Procedure 001-028 for the Signature, ISX and QSX15 series engine.</p>","tsb101325":"<h2>New Hand Hole Cover</h2><h3>Warranty Statement</h3><p>The information in this document has no effect on present warranty coverage or repair practices, nor does it authorize TRP or Campaign actions.</p><h3>Contents</h3><p>This service/parts topic introduces a new hand hole cover for the QSK45 and QSK60 series engines. The new hand hole cover is introduced as part of the new crankcase breather system that replaces the conventional head deck and crankcase side mounted breather arrangement.</p><p>The new hand hole cover went into production at the Daventry Engine Plant from Engine Serial Number first of 33152044 for the QSK45 and 33153219 for the QSK60.</p>","tsb101326":"<h2>New Single Box Breather Assembly</h2><h3>Warranty Statement</h3><p>The information in this document has no effect on present warranty coverage or repair practices, nor does it authorize TRP or Campaign actions.</p><h3>Contents</h3><p>This service/parts topic introduces a new crankcase breather system for the QSK45 and QSK60 series engines. This design replaces the conventional head deck and crankcase side mounted breather arrangement in order to alleviate oil carryover which the conventional head deck and crankcase side mounted breather system exhibits.</p><p>The new crankcase breather system went into production at the Daventry Engine Plant from Engine Serial Number first of 33152044 for the QSK45 and 33153219 for the QSK60.</p><p>Information contained within this topic covers the removal and installation instructions for the new single box breather system with the Phase 2.0 wiring harness only.</p><p>Figure 1. shows the new design fitted to a QSK60 series engine (QSK45 series engines are similar).</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/03600098.png\" alt=\"03600098.png\"></figure><p>Figure 1.</p>","tsb101331":"<h2>Using Heat Indicator Markers to Check Exhaust Port Temperature.</h2><h3>Warranty Statement</h3><p>The information in this document has no effect on present warranty coverage or repair practices, nor does it authorize TRP or Campaign actions.</p><h3>Contents</h3><p>This service/parts topic is adding a service tool and creating procedures for using heat indicator markers to locate cold or misfiring engine cylinders.</p><div class=\"callout note\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">i</span>Note · Примечание</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>This is a product improvement and <b>not</b> subject to campaign.</p></div></div>","tsb101334":"<h2>Revision to Procedure 001-054</h2><h3>Warranty Statement</h3><p>The information in this document has no effect on present warranty coverage or repair practices, nor does it authorize TRP or Campaign actions.</p><h3>Contents</h3><p>This Service/Parts Topic revises Procedure 001-054, Piston and Connecting Rod Assembly.</p>","tsb101335":"<h2>Revision to Procedure 007-035, Lubricating Oil Suction Tube (Block-Mounted)</h2><h3>Warranty Statement</h3><p>The information in this document has no effect on present warranty coverage or repair practices, nor does it authorize TRP or Campaign actions.</p><h3>Contents</h3><p>This is product improvement and is <b>not</b> subject to campaign.</p>","tsb101341":"<h2>Revised Procedure 006-026, Injector</h2><h3>Warranty Statement</h3><p>The information in this document has no effect on present warranty coverage or repair practices, nor does it authorize TRP or Campaign actions.</p><h3>Contents</h3><p>This Service/Parts Topic revises Procedure, 006-026, Injector.</p>","tsb101344":"<h2>Revision to Procedure 022-001, Service Tools (Injectors and Fuel Lines)</h2><h3>Warranty Statement</h3><p>The information in this document has no effect on present warranty coverage or repair practices, nor does it authorize TRP or Campaign actions.</p><h3>Contents</h3><p>This Service/Parts Topic revises Procedure 022-001, Service Tools (Injectors and Fuel Lines).</p>","tsb101350":"<h2>New and Revised Procedures for Engine Cooling System</h2><h3>Warranty Statement</h3><p>The information in this document has no effect on present warranty coverage or repair practices, nor does it authorize TRP or Campaign actions.</p><h3>Contents</h3><p>This Service/Parts Topic introduces and revises Procedures, Coolant Thermostat 008-013, Cooling System 008-018, Radiator Hoses 008-045, Sea Water Pump 008-057, Sea Water Strainer 008-067, Sea Water System Diagnostic, 008-103, and Sea Water Hoses 008-104.</p>","tsb101364":"<h2>Centinel™ Feature Update</h2><h3>Warranty Statement</h3><p>The information in this document has no effect on present warranty coverage or repair practices, nor does it authorize TRP or Campaign actions.</p><h3>Contents</h3><p>This Service/Parts Topic revises the Centinel™ Feature.</p>","tsb101365":"<h2>Centinel™ System Description</h2><h3>Warranty Statement</h3><p>The information in this document has no effect on present warranty coverage or repair practices, nor does it authorize TRP or Campaign actions.</p><h3>Contents</h3><p>This Service/Parts Topic updates the System Description (209-001) of the Centinel™ Master Repair Manual.</p>","tsb101366":"<h2>Centinel™ Oil Lines</h2><h3>Warranty Statement</h3><p>The information in this document has no effect on present warranty coverage or repair practices, nor does it authorize TRP or Campaign actions.</p><h3>Contents</h3><p>This Service/Parts Topic introduces procedure <a class=\"lnk doc\" href=\"#/doc/96-007-088\">007-088</a> and <a class=\"lnk doc\" href=\"#/doc/96-007-089\">007-089</a> for Centinel™ Installations.</p>","tsb101387":"<h2>New Fan Idler Dust Shields, update to Procedure 008-030, Fan Drive Idler Pulley Assembly</h2><h3>Warranty Statement</h3><p>The information in this document has no effect on present warranty coverage or repair practices, nor does it authorize TRP or Campaign actions.</p><h3>Contents</h3><p>This Service/Parts Topic announces a fan idler bearing dust shield, Part Number 3635105, for the QSK45 and QSK60 engines and updates Procedure 008-030, Fan Drive Idler Pulley Assembly.</p><p>The dust shield reduces the possibility of fan belt tensioner issues due to dust ingress to the fan idler bearing.</p><p>The component went into production at the Daventry Engine Plant in July, 2003.</p><p>The engine serial number first is 33154312.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/08600527.png\" alt=\"08600527.png\"></figure><p>Dust Shield, Part Number 3635105.</p><div class=\"callout note\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">i</span>Note · Примечание</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>This is a product improvement, <b>not</b> subject to campaign.</p></div></div><p>The new dust shields are installed on either side of the idler pulley as part of the Pivot Arm Assembly (1).</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/08600324.png\" alt=\"08600324.png\"></figure><div class=\"callout note\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">i</span>Note · Примечание</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>When using dust shields with pivot arm assembly, Part Number 4066540, the use of the key shaft is <b>not</b> required. Fan idler bearings should be replaced with Part Number 4016800.</p></div></div><p>The bearings (4) mount on the idler shaft (1). The two dust shields (3), packed with grease, mount to the bearings; one at the spacer (6) side, and the other at the shaft shoulder (1) side.</p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Pivot Arm Assembly Parts Matrix</th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Item Number</td><td>Description</td><td>Part Number</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>Idler shaft</td><td>4066386</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Idler pulley</td><td>4016914</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Idler bearing</td><td>4016800</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Dust shield</td><td>3635105</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>Spacer ring</td><td>4001568</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>Spring</td><td>3171146</td></tr></tbody></table></div><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/08600519.png\" alt=\"08600519.png\"></figure>","tsb101390":"<h2>Revision to Procedure 001-014, Connecting Rod</h2><h3>Warranty Statement</h3><p>The information in this document has no effect on present warranty coverage or repair practices, nor does it authorize TRP or Campaign actions.</p><h3>Contents</h3><p>This Service/Parts Topic documents revisions to Procedure 001-014, Connecting Rod in the C8.3 Gas Base Engine Troubleshooting and Repair Manual and C Series Troubleshooting and Repair Manual.</p>","tsb101392":"<h2>Cylinder Head Gasket Oil Passage Plugs</h2><h3>Warranty Statement</h3><p>The information in this document has no effect on present warranty coverage or repair practices, nor does it authorize TRP or Campaign actions.</p><h3>Contents</h3><p>This Service/Parts Topic updates Procedure <a class=\"lnk doc\" href=\"#/doc/10-002-004-tr\">002-004</a> for the Signature, ISX and QSX15 Engines Troubleshooting and Repair Manual, Bulletin <a class=\"lnk man\" href=\"#/manual/3666239\">3666239</a>.</p>","tsb101395":"<h2>Lubricating Oil Cooler Leak Test</h2><h3>Warranty Statement</h3><p>The information in this document has no effect on present warranty coverage or repair practices, nor does it authorize TRP or Campaign actions.</p><h3>Contents</h3><p>This Service/Parts Topic modifies Procedure 007-003 of the Signature, ISX, QSX15 Troubleshooting and Repair Manual, Bulletin <a class=\"lnk man\" href=\"#/manual/3666239\">3666239</a>, to include additional instructions for leak testing the lubricating oil cooler assembly. Leak testing the oil cooler assembly can be useful in troubleshooting coolant in lubricating oil conditions, testing the element to cooler housing gaskets and testing the elements.</p><p>This Service/Parts Topic also introduces a new service tool, lubricating oil cooler leak test kit, Part Number 4918200. This kit is necessary to leak test the oil cooler as an assembly.</p>","tsb101398":"<h2>Revised Engine Speed Surges at Low or High Idle Symptom Tree.</h2><h3>Warranty Statement</h3><p>The information in this document has no effect on present warranty coverage or repair practices, nor does it authorize TRP or Campaign actions.</p><h3>Contents</h3><p>This service/parts topic corrects Base Engine Troubleshooting and Repair Procedure t066 Engine Speed Surges at Low or High Idle.</p><p>The eighth step reads: Engine position sensor (ESP) or circuit is malfunctioning. Check the engine position sensor.</p><p>Should read: Engine speed sensor (ESS) or circuit is malfunctioning. Check the engine speed sensor and circuit.</p><p>Reference Troubleshooting and Repair Manual, QSK45 and QSK60 Series Engines, Bulletin 3666261.</p><h3>Document History</h3>","tsb101405":"<h2>Revision to Procedure 007-003, Lubricating Oil Cooler</h2><h3>Warranty Statement</h3><p>The information in this document has no effect on present warranty coverage or repair practices, nor does it authorize TRP or Campaign actions.</p><h3>Contents</h3><p>This Service/Parts Topic is a revision to Procedure 007-003, Lubricating Oil Cooler. The leak test kit, Part Number 3823876, has been included in the leak test step. The torque values for the lubricating oil cooler capscrews have changed in the install step.</p>","tsb101410":"<h2>Procedure 002-004, Cylinder Head</h2><h3>Warranty Statement</h3><p>The information in this document has no effect on present warranty coverage or repair practices, nor does it authorize TRP or Campaign actions.</p><h3>Contents</h3><p>This Service/Parts Topic revises Procedure 002-004, Cylinder Head to include the intake valve recession limits.</p><p>In the past few years, there has been an issue with conflicting information in regard to ISM/QSM intake valve recession limits.</p><p>As field locations have worked their way through valve beat-in issues, it has become apparent that there is some conflicting information within Service Parts Topics, PITS documents and old Thursday Notes.</p><p>Valve beat-in and associated progressive damage such as cam bushing failures, cam lobe failures, cam follower damage, and valve failures can be significantly influenced by valve seals, lube oils, and maintenance practices.</p><p>During the years 1998 and 1999, a group of automotive engines were built with dry valve seals. The Engine Serial Numbers (ESNs) and dates are given below:</p><ul><li>Beginning with ESN 34891154 (December 7, 1997)</li><li>Ending with ESN 34929771 (September 20, 1998)</li><li>Engines built with the dry seal can experience intake seat wear (beat-in) due to lack of lubrication during extended periods of high boost operation. This beat-in leads to negative intake lash that causes the valve train to be continuously loaded with an additional load spike during combustion. The contact stresses on the opening ramp lead to asperity scale spalling (frosting) and camshaft lobe failure.</li></ul><p>Several improvements have been made since that time period.</p><ul><li>Improved valve seal (Part Number <a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/4003966\">4003966</a>) went into production on September 20, 1998.</li><li>J120V valve seat inserts were implemented in production in the intake positions with ESN 35062901 (June 24, 2001). J120V valve seat inserts had already been used in the exhaust locations.</li></ul>","tsb101411":"<h2>Revisions to Procedures 001-043 and 001-054</h2><h3>Warranty Statement</h3><p>The information in this document has no effect on present warranty coverage or repair practices, nor does it authorize TRP or Campaign actions.</p><h3>Contents</h3><p>The purpose of this Service Parts Topic is to revise Procedure 001-043 Piston and Procedure 001-054 Piston and Connecting Rod Assemby.</p>","tsb101430":"<h2>Revision to Procedure 010-031, Air Intake Restriction</h2><h3>Warranty Statement</h3><p>The information in this document has no effect on present warranty coverage or repair practices, nor does it authorize TRP or Campaign actions.</p><h3>Contents</h3><p>This Service/Parts Topic is a revision to Procedure 010-031, Air Intake Restriction. The air intake restriction specification was updated.</p>","tsb101433":"<h2>Revision to Procedure 010-033, Turbocharger</h2><h3>Warranty Statement</h3><p>The information in this document has no effect on present warranty coverage or repair practices, nor does it authorize TRP or Campaign actions.</p><h3>Contents</h3><p>The purpose of this Service/Parts Topic is to update Procedure 010-033 with improved troubleshooting and repair steps.</p>","tsb101439":"<h2>Turbocharger Leak Troubleshooting Tree</h2><h3>Warranty Statement</h3><p>The information in this document has no effect on present warranty coverage or repair practices, nor does it authorize TRP or Campaign actions.</p><h3>Contents</h3><p>The purpose of this Service/Parts Topic is to update Troubleshooting Tree T122 with improved troubleshooting logic.</p>","tsb101444":"<h2>TELC Plus Silicates in Cummins Engines</h2><h3>Warranty Statement</h3><p>The information in this document has no effect on present warranty coverage or repair practices, nor does it authorize TRP or Campaign actions.</p><h3>Contents</h3><p>For Cummins engines that are using ChevronTexaco® or Shell® Rotella Extended Life Coolant (TELC) Plus Silicates</p><p>Cummins recommends Heavy Duty engines and High Horsepower engines using ChevronTexaco, Shell Rotella, and their private label counterpart Extended Life OAT Coolants, which do <b>not</b> meet the elastomer compatibility section of CES 14603 are acceptable for extended service interval use assuming the initial coolant fill requirements were met from the vehicles&#x27; original equipment manufacturer (OEM).</p><p>Heavy Duty and High Horsepower engine overhauls or repairs involving the replacement of the following components, using this OAT coolant <b>must</b> discard the coolant and replace with new coolant.</p><ul><li>rocker lever housing gasket</li><li>oil cooler housing gasket</li><li>cylinder head gasket</li><li>thermostat housing gasket.</li></ul><p>If the replacement coolant is ChevronTexaco, Shell Rotella, or their private label counterpart Extended Life OAT Coolants, which do <b>not</b> meet the elastomer compatibility section of CES 14603, then the coolant <b>must</b> be treated by adding one 8 ounce bottle of liquid silicate fluid for every 12 gallons of total coolant system volume. It is critical to <b>not</b> overtreat the coolant with silicate fluid.</p><p>To obtain order forms or ask questions relative to ordering the silicate fluid:</p><p>Phone: 1-800-346-9041</p><p>Fax: 1-800-876-5317</p><p>Mail To: Silicate Fluid Order Program</p><p>PO Box 27388</p><p>Houston, TX 77227-7388</p><p>For technical questions on the Silicate Fluid, please call 1-800-782-7852 and choose option 3.</p><p>Additional important information is included in Figure 1 (Global Customer Bulletin) and Figure 2 (Frequently Asked Questions).</p><p>If there are any additional engine-related questions, please call Cummins Technical Hotline at 1-800-DIESELS.</p><p>Figure 1:</p><p>Global Customer Bulletin</p><p>This communication is being sent to update customers of Cummins Inc., ChevronTexaco, Shell Rotella, and Equilon® Lubricants on the issue of gasket coolant leakage in Cummins N14 engines, when factor filled with ChevronTexaco, Shell Rotella Extended Life OAT Coolants.</p><p>As stated in previous Customer Bulletins, Cummins, ChevronTexaco, Shell Rotella, and Equilon have been working together to find solutions.</p><p>Cummins has approved ChevronTexaco, Shell Rotella Extended Life OAT Coolants as a factory fill antifreeze/coolant in the following Cummins engine worldwide:</p><p>ISB, ISB e and B series engines</p><p>ISC and C series engines</p><p>ISL series engines</p><p>Cummins has approved ChevronTexaco, Shell Rotella Extended Life OAT Coolants plus a one-time addition of silicate as a factory fill antifreeze/coolant in the following Cummins engines worldwide:</p><p>N14 and N14 Plus engines</p><p>ISX and Signature engines</p><p>ISM, M11 and M11 Plus engines</p><p>High Horsepower Engines</p><p>The use of silicates as a preventive maintenance recommendation for the ISM, ISX engines and High Horsepower engines.</p><p>Please contact your local Equilon or ChevronTexaco, Shell representative for details on obtaining the proper silicate product and dosage.</p><p>This coolant may be used in off-road engines analogous to their over-the-road counterparts as outlined above.</p><p>We will jointly collaborate to determine what additional improvements might be possible, and will communicate the results of this work should new information become available.</p><p>Figure 2: Frequently Asked Questions</p><p>Q: I have a C (or B) series Cummins engine which was factory filled with ChevronTexaco, Shell Rotella Extended Life OAT Coolant. Do I need to add &#x27;Silicate Fluid for Cummins Engines <b>only</b> &#x27; (&#x27;Silicate Fluid&#x27;) to my coolant system?</p><p>A: No.</p><p>Q: I have a C (or B) series Cummins engine which was factory filled with ChevronTexaco, Shell Rotella Extended Life OAT Coolant Extended Life OAT Coolant. I have already added Silicate Fluid to my cooling system. Should I drain and flush now that I find that the addition of silicates was <b>not</b> necessary?</p><p>A: No.</p><p>Q: I have already added Silicate Fluid to my M11 which was factory filled with ChevronTexaco, Shell Rotella Extended Life OAT Coolant. Should I add more Silicate Fluid?</p><p>A: No.</p><p>Q: I have an N14 engine which was factory filled with ChevronTexaco, Shell Rotella Extended Life OAT Coolant and a rocker housing gasket is leaking. Can I use ChevronTexaco, Shell Rotella Extended Life OAT Coolant after I have the gasket replaced?</p><p>Yes. Just drain and dispose of the used coolant. Replace the rocker housing gasket and refill the cooling system with ChevronTexaco, Shell Rotella Extended Life OAT Coolant Extended Life OAT Coolant 50/50. Add one 8 ounce bottle of silicate fluid for every 12 gallons of total coolant system volume. Circulate the engine coolant for 15 to 20 minutes, or until the thermostat opens. Check the freeze point and adjust to -37°C [-34°F].</p><p>Q: I have an ISX which was factory filled with ChevronTexaco, Shell Rotella Extended Life OAT Coolants. I have already added Silicate Fluid to the cooling system. My coolant inadvertently became contaminated and I plan to flush and refill with new ChevronTexaco, Shell Rotella Extended Life OAT Coolant. Do I need to treat again with Silicate Fluid?</p><p>A: No. If the original gaskets remained in place, adding Silicate Fluid again will <b>not</b> be required.</p><p>Q: I have heard that using ChevronTexaco, Shell Rotella Extended Life OAT Coolant in Cummins engine was responsible for rocker housing gasket failures. Is this true?</p><p>A: A joint team of Cummins, Equilon, and ChevronTexaco scientists and engineers determined that a unique combination of circumstances needed to be present in order for the rocker housing gasket seepage to occur.</p><p>Q: I have heard that the use of ChevronTexaco, Shell Rotella Extended Life OAT Coolant can lead to catastrophic failure, including oil solidification (gelation).</p><p>A: After more than a year of intensive cooperative testing, Cummins has approved the use of ChevronTexaco, Shell Rotella Extended Life OAT Coolant in all Mid-Range, Heavy Duty, and High Horsepower engines (either with or without the one-time addition of Silicate Fluid, depending on the engine model, at the time of the factory fill). Cummins would <b>not</b> provide this endorsement if there was any concern that the use of ChevronTexaco, Shell Rotella Extended Life OAT Coolant would lead to catastrophic failure. Oil and coolant <b>must never</b> be mixed, regardless of the engine make or coolant formulation. The presence of coolant in the engine oil represents a serious situation that <b>must</b> be remedied immediately. Oil testing is the best way to determine if coolant has mixed with the engine oil. Coolant intrusion into crankcase oil can lead to sludge, solidification, and potential engine failure, regardless of the coolant formulation being used. This includes conventional, fully formulated, extended service, and extended life coolants.</p><p>Q: My Cummins N14 engine was factory filled with ChevronTexaco, Shell Rotella Extended Life OAT Coolant. If I have a rocker housing gasket leak, will my Cummins warranty cover the repair?</p><p>A: Yes. After the rocker gasket is repaired, if you choose to continue using ChevronTexaco, Shell Rotella Extended Life OAT Coolant, be sure the repairing shop adds the Silicate Fluid and new 50/50 TELC to your cooling system.</p><p>Q: What has changed? Why is it okay to use the coolant now when it was once <b>not</b> okay?</p><p>A: Intense investigations proved that more than one factor caused the failure of a certain percentage of rocker housing gaskets in Cummins N14 engines. The addition of the Silicate Fluid is a preventive maintenance step recommended by ChevronTexaco, Equilon, and Cummins to reduce the possibility of seal degradation.</p><p>Q: How does one application of the silicate additive provide the required protection for engines such as the N14?</p><p>A: The effectiveness of silicates has been demonstrated in the laboratory, dyno, and field studies, and has been shown to be effective in extending the life of seals in Cummins engines at the recommended dosage level. It is believed that silicates create a protective barrier at the surface of the seal that does <b>not</b> allow free contact of coolant and the seal surface.</p><p>Q: Can ChevronTexaco, Shell Rotella Extended Life OAT Coolants be used with Cummins High-Horsepower industrial engines such as the QSK/KV, the V28, or the QSK78?</p><p>A: Yes. ChevronTexaco, Shell Rotella Extended Life OAT Coolants, which do <b>not</b> meet the elastometer compatibility section of CES 14603 are acceptable for extended service interval use in High-Horsepower industrial engines, assuming the initial coolant fill requirements were met from the original equipment manufacturer (OEM).</p><p>Q: My engine, which has ChevronTexaco, Shell Rotella Extended Life OAT Coolants Plus Silicates as coolant is being repaired. Assuming the coolant is <b>not</b> contaminated and in good condition, can it be reused?</p><p>A: The coolant can be reused if the repair procedure does <b>not</b> require the replacement of any cooling system gaskets. If any cooling system gaskets are replaced, the coolant <b>must</b> be replaced with new 50/50 TELC and one 8 ounce bottle of silicate fluid per every 12 gallons of total cooling system volume.</p><p>Q: How can I get more information on the maintenance requirements of ChevronTexaco, Shell Rotella Extended Life OAT Coolants and ChevronTexaco, Shell Rotella Extended Life OAT Coolants Plus Silicates? What tests are required to be sure the coolant&#x27;s condition and operation meet the engine&#x27;s needs?</p><p>A: Call 1-800-782-7852 and choose option 3 for technical information. If you would like to test TELC for inhibitor level at your site, use the Extended Life Coolant Test Strip (ELCTS). These test kits can be ordered from Analyst Inc. at 1-800-336-3637.</p><div class=\"callout note\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">i</span>Note · Примечание</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>It is very important to maintain freeze protection of the coolant. The optimum freeze protection is -37°C [-34°F] even if the engine is located in warm-weather climates.</p></div></div><p>Q: If an N14 rocker housing gasket is being replaced, will silicates need to be added regardless of mileage or hours since silicates were originally introduced to the engine?</p><p>A: Yes. It is recommended that the coolant be replaced with ChevronTexaco, Shell Rotella Extended Life OAT Coolants, 50/50, and one 8 ounce bottle of silicate fluid per every 12 gallons of total cooling system volume.</p><p>Q: Can excessive amounts of silicate fluid in the coolant induce issues that could impact engine performance?</p><p>A: If overtreatment is suspected, drain and flush the cooling system. Fill the cooling system with new coolant and add one 8 ounce bottle of silicate fluid per every 12 gallons of total cooling system volume.</p>","tsb101446":"<h2>Revision to Procedure 019-063, Internal Actuator Wiring Harness</h2><h3>Warranty Statement</h3><p>The information in this document has no effect on present warranty coverage or repair practices, nor does it authorize TRP or Campaign actions.</p><h3>Contents</h3><p>This Service/Parts Topic revises procedure 019-063, Internal Actuator Wiring Harness.</p>","tsb101447":"<h2>Revision to Fault Code 482, Low Fuel Pressure</h2><h3>Warranty Statement</h3><p>The information in this document has no effect on present warranty coverage or repair practices, nor does it authorize TRP or Campaign actions.</p><h3>Contents</h3><p>This Service/Parts Topic revises Fault Code 482 for the ISX Series engine.</p>","tsb101454":"<h2>Revision To Procedure 022-001</h2><h3>Warranty Statement</h3><p>The information in this document has no effect on present warranty coverage or repair practices, nor does it authorize TRP or Campaign actions.</p><h3>Contents</h3><p>This Service/Parts topic revises the service tools page, Procedure 022-001 in the Troubleshooting and Repair Manual Signature, ISX, QSX15 Engines, Bulletin <a class=\"lnk man\" href=\"#/manual/3666239\">3666239</a>.</p>","tsb101458":"<h2>Rear Crankshaft Seal Remove and Install, Procedure 001-024</h2><h3>Warranty Statement</h3><p>The information in this document has no effect on present warranty coverage or repair practices, nor does it authorize TRP or Campaign actions.</p><h3>Contents</h3><p>This Service/Parts Topic revises the rear crankshaft seal Procedure 001-024 on the L10, L10G, M11, ISM, ISM e, and QSM11 Series engines. The revised procedure updates the oil seal removal and installation for Non-REPTO Dry Lip Seal, Non-REPTO Dry Unitized Seal, and REPTO Dry Seal applications. This Service/Parts Topic does <b>not</b> change the rear crankshaft seal or the rear crankshaft seal Part Number.</p>","tsb101461":"<h2>Flywheel Tolerance Correction</h2><h3>Warranty Statement</h3><p>The information in this document has no effect on present warranty coverage or repair practices, nor does it authorize TRP or Campaign actions.</p><h3>Contents</h3><p>This service/parts topic is to inform the field of a correction to flywheel runout tolerances listed in Section 16 - Mounting Applications for the QSK45 and QSK60 engines.</p>","tsb101464":"<h2>Revised Lubricating Oil Filter (Spin-On), Procedure 007-013</h2><h3>Warranty Statement</h3><p>The information in this document has no effect on present warranty coverage or repair practices, nor does it authorize TRP or Campaign actions.</p><h3>Contents</h3><p>This Service/Parts Topic revises Procedure 007-013 in the Troubleshooting and Repair Manual. Two new oil filter wrenches are available.</p>","tsb101465":"<h2>Revised Tool Requirements- General Information, Procedure 102-004</h2><h3>Warranty Statement</h3><p>The information in this document has no effect on present warranty coverage or repair practices, nor does it authorize TRP or Campaign actions.</p><h3>Contents</h3><p>This Service/Parts Topic revises Procedure 102-004 in the Operation and Maintenance Manuals.</p>","tsb101467":"<h2>Revision to Procedure 019-110 and Procedure 019-111</h2><h3>Warranty Statement</h3><p>The information in this document has no effect on present warranty coverage or repair practices, nor does it authorize TRP or Campaign actions.</p><h3>Contents</h3><p>This Service/Parts Topic updates Procedure 019-110, Fueling Actuator and Procedure 019-111, Timing Actuator to include the INSITE™ electronic service tool test procedure used to determine if an actuator has failed.</p>","tsb101469":"<h2>Revised Cylinder Liner Diameter Measurement</h2><h3>Warranty Statement</h3><p>The information in this document has no effect on present warranty coverage or repair practices, nor does it authorize TRP or Campaign actions.</p><h3>Contents</h3><p>This Service/Parts Topic updates a change made to the QSK45 and QSK60 series engines. The topic provides details on Cylinder Liner Inner Diameters when installed in an engine block.</p><p>Diametrical measurements <b>must</b> be taken at the positions specified in Figure 1 and Table 1.</p><ul><li>Position 1 = 20 mm [0.8 in] from top of liner</li><li>Position 2 = 115 mm [4.5 in] from top of liner</li><li>Position 3 = 205 mm [8.0 in] from top of liner.</li></ul><p>At each specified position, two measurements <b>must</b> be taken. The measurements <b>must</b> be 90 degrees apart. Ensure that the measurements taken fall within the limits specified within the table below.</p><p>The inner diameter <b>must</b> be <b>no more</b> than 0.064 mm [0.0025 in], out of round at each location.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/01600303.png\" alt=\"01600303.png\"></figure><p>Figure 1</p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Table 1. Cylinder Liner Inside Diameter.</th><th></th><th></th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Reference Position</td><td>Distance from Top of Liner</td><td>Maximum Inner Diameter</td><td>Minimum Inner Diameter</td><td>Maximum Liner Ovality</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>20 mm [0.8 in]</td><td>158.75 mm [6.25 in]</td><td>158.65 mm [6.246 in]</td><td>0.064 mm [0.0025 in]</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>115 mm [4.5 in]</td><td>158.785 mm [6.2513 in]</td><td>158.725 mm [6.2490 in]</td><td>0.064 mm [0.0025 in]</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>205 mm [8.0 in]</td><td>158.785 mm [6.2513 in]</td><td>158.725 mm [6.2490 in]</td><td>0.064 mm [0.0025 in]</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"callout note\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">i</span>Note · Примечание</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>This is a product improvement and is <b>not</b> subject to campaign.</p></div></div>","tsb101470":"<h2>Lubricating Oil Filter Head Adapter, Procedure 007-018 and Lubricating Oil Pan, Procedure 007-025</h2><h3>Warranty Statement</h3><p>The information in this document has no effect on present warranty coverage or repair practices, nor does it authorize TRP or Campaign actions.</p><h3>Contents</h3><p>This Service/Parts Topic revises Procedure 007-018 and 007-025 in the Troubleshooting and Repair Manual. In Procedure 007-018, an improved filter head adapter replacer, Part Number 4918222 is available. In Procedure 007-025, the old sealant, Part Number 3823494 was replaced with a new form-in-place gasket compound sealant, Part Number 3164067. The new sealant, Part Number 3164067, was released in order to reflect changes in sealants used at the production plants.</p>","tsb101471":"<h2>Revision to Procedure 019-110 and Procedure 019-111</h2><h3>Warranty Statement</h3><p>The information in this document has no effect on present warranty coverage or repair practices, nor does it authorize TRP or Campaign actions.</p><h3>Contents</h3><p>This Service/Parts Topic updates Procedure 019-110, Fueling Actuator and Procedure 019-111, Timing Actuator to include the INSITE™ electronic service tool test procedure used to determine if an actuator has failed.</p>","tsb101479":"<h2>Revision to Procedure 008-062, Water Pump</h2><h3>Warranty Statement</h3><p>The information in this document has no effect on present warranty coverage or repair practices, nor does it authorize TRP or Campaign actions.</p><h3>Contents</h3>","tsb101483":"<h2>Revision to Procedure 005-045, Fuel Lift Pump</h2><h3>Warranty Statement</h3><p>The information in this document has no effect on present warranty coverage or repair practices, nor does it authorize TRP or Campaign actions.</p><h3>Contents</h3><p>This Service/Parts Topic revises Procedure 005-045, Fuel Lift Pump.</p>","tsb101485":"<h2>Revised Engine Difficult to Start Symptom Trees</h2><h3>Warranty Statement</h3><p>The information in this document has no effect on present warranty coverage or repair practices, nor does it authorize TRP or Campaign actions.</p><h3>Contents</h3><p>This Service/Parts Topic revises the following symptom trees:</p><ul><li>Engine Difficult to Start or Will Not Start (Exhaust Smoke)</li><li>Engine Difficult to Start or Will Not Start (No Exhaust Smoke).</li></ul>","tsb101486":"<h2>New Lubricating Oil Transfer Tube and Oil Transfer Connection</h2><h3>Warranty Statement</h3><p>The information in this document has no effect on present warranty coverage or repair practices, nor does it authorize TRP or Campaign actions.</p><h3>Contents</h3><p>This Service/Parts Topic is to introduce a revised lubricating oil pump discharge tube and oil transfer connection. The new discharge tube and connections have been introduced to improve durability of the system components. The new arrangement is only compatible with those engines that have lubricating oil pumps, Part Number 4100711 for the QSK60, and Part Number 4100710 for the QSK45, and can be used as a direct replacement. Engines that have lubricating oil pump part numbers different to those above remain unaffected and no action is required as a result of this topic. The new arrangement went into production at the Daventry Engine Plant on 8th of March 2004. The ESN first was 33156055.</p><p>Revised Arrangement.</p><p>The revised arrangement consists of a connection elbow fixed to the discharge port of the lube pump, a straight discharge tube and another connection fixed to the engine block. The lubricating oil pump remains unchanged, QSK 60 (Part Number 4100711), and QSK 45 (Part Number 4100710).</p><div class=\"callout note\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">i</span>Note · Примечание</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>This is a product improvement <b>not</b> subject to campaign.</p></div></div><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/07601041.png\" alt=\"07601041.png\"></figure><p>Figure 1</p><p>Table 1 below, lists the parts that <b>must</b> be ordered when fitting the new components on QSK45/60engines.</p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Table 1, Part Required (See Figure 1)</th><th></th><th></th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Reference No.</td><td>QSK60 Parts</td><td>QSK45 Parts</td><td>Quantity</td><td>Description</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>4916879</td><td>4916879</td><td>1</td><td>Sleeve</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/3636362\">3636362</a></td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/3636362\">3636362</a></td><td>1</td><td>Oil transfer connection</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/3914407\">3914407</a></td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/3914407\">3914407</a></td><td>3</td><td>Screw, hex flange head cap</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/4916878\">4916878</a></td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/4916878\">4916878</a></td><td>1</td><td>Tube, lub oil transfer</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/3089886\">3089886</a></td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/3089886\">3089886</a></td><td>2</td><td>Seal, rectangular</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/4916874\">4916874</a></td><td>4916875</td><td>1</td><td>Connection, lub oil</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/3089016\">3089016</a></td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/3089016\">3089016</a></td><td>1</td><td>Screw, hex flange head cap</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/3089234\">3089234</a></td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/3089234\">3089234</a></td><td>1</td><td>Screw, hex flange head cap</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/3920447\">3920447</a></td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/3920447\">3920447</a></td><td>2</td><td>Screw, hex flange head cap</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/3902460\">3902460</a></td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/3902460\">3902460</a></td><td>6</td><td>Screw, hex flange head cap</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Identification</p><p>S-Shape Type</p><p>Fitted to lubricating oil pump part numbers:</p><p>QSK45 - 4066027</p><p>QSK60 - 4065652</p><p>Arrangement consists of lubricating oil pump connection (3) and block connection (1), with a steel tube (2) bridging the connections (1) and (3).</p><p>Connection (1) has three mounting capscrews. Steel tube has slight bend in its section.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/07600388.png\" alt=\"07600388.png\"></figure><p>Elbow Type</p><p>Fitted to lubricating oil pump part numbers:</p><p>QSK45 - 4100710</p><p>QSK60 - 4100711</p><p>Arrangement consists of block connection (1), with a steel tube (2) which bridges from block connections (1) directly to lubricating oil pump. Transfer tube bracing is also present.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/07601042.png\" alt=\"07601042.png\"></figure><p>Straight Type</p><p>Fitted to lubricating oil pump part numbers:</p><p>QSK45 - 4100710</p><p>QSK60 - 4100711</p><p>Arrangement consists of lubricating oil pump connection (2) and block connection (6), with a steel tube (4) bridging the connections (2) and (6).</p><p>Connection (6) has four mounting capscrews. Steel tube is straight in section.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/07601043.png\" alt=\"07601043.png\"></figure>","tsb101505":"<h2>Revision To Troubleshooting Tree</h2><h3>Warranty Statement</h3><p>The information in this document has no effect on present warranty coverage or repair practices, nor does it authorize TRP or Campaign actions.</p><h3>Contents</h3><p>The purpose of this Service/Parts Topic is to revise Troubleshooting Tree T103-55.</p>","tsb101508":"<h2>Lubricating Oil Pump</h2><h3>Warranty Statement</h3><p>The information in this document has no effect on present warranty coverage or repair practices, nor does it authorize TRP or Campaign actions.</p><h3>Contents</h3><p>The purpose of this Service/Parts Topic is to introduce rebuild instructions for the lubricating oil pump for Signature, ISX, and QSX15 engines.</p>","tsb101514":"<h2>Undersized Main Bearings</h2><h3>Warranty Statement</h3><p>The information in this document has no effect on present warranty coverage or repair practices, nor does it authorize TRP or Campaign actions.</p><h3>Contents</h3><p>This Service Parts Topic introduces undersized main bearings for the M11, ISM, and QSM11 series engines. Undersized main bearings can be used as a repair option for main cap fretting instead of cylinder block replacement. Undersized main bearings are approved for National Overhaul Warranty (N.O.W.).</p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Parts Table</th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Kit Description</td><td>Part Number</td></tr><tr><td>Undersized Upper and Lower Main Bearing Kit</td><td>4089868</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Kit Contents</th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Part Description</td><td>Part Number</td><td>Quantity</td></tr><tr><td>Undersized Upper Main Bearing Shell</td><td>4920494</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>Undersized Lower Main Bearing Shell</td><td>4920495</td><td>1</td></tr></tbody></table></div>","tsb101518":"<h2>New Fan Idler Dust Shields</h2><h3>Warranty Statement</h3><p>The information in this document has no effect on present warranty coverage or repair practices, nor does it authorize TRP or Campaign actions.</p><h3>Contents</h3><p>This Service/Parts Topic supersedes topic 04T8-6. Replace 04T8-6 with this topic.</p><p>This Service/Parts Topic announces a fan idler bearing dust shield, Part Number 3635105, for the QSK45 and QSK60 engines and updates Procedure 008-030, Fan Drive Idler Pulley Assembly.</p><p>The dust shield reduces the possibility of fan belt tensioner issues due to dust ingress to the fan idler bearing.</p><p>The component went into production at the Daventry Engine Plant in July, 2003.</p><p>The engine serial number first is 33154312.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/08600527.png\" alt=\"08600527.png\"></figure><p>Dust Shield, Part Number 3635105.</p><div class=\"callout note\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">i</span>Note · Примечание</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>This is a product improvement, <b>not</b> subject to campaign.</p></div></div><div class=\"callout note\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">i</span>Note · Примечание</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>This does <b>not</b> apply to Komatsu WA1200 front-end loaders equipped with a QSK60 engine with fan drive option FA 6165-06. The fan belt pulley and bearings on WA1200 are <b>not</b> the same as on other industrial QSK60 engines, and therefore can <b>not</b> use the dust shields Part Number 3635105.</p></div></div><p>The new dust shields are installed on either side of the idler pulley as part of the Pivot Arm Assembly (1).</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/08600324.png\" alt=\"08600324.png\"></figure><div class=\"callout note\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">i</span>Note · Примечание</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>When using dust shields with pivot arm assembly, Part Number 4066540, the use of the key shaft is <b>not</b> required. Fan idler bearings should be replaced with Part Number 4016800.</p></div></div><p>The bearings (4) mount on the idler shaft (1). The two dust shields (3), packed with grease, mount to the bearings; one at the spacer (6) side, and the other at the shaft shoulder (1) side.</p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Pivot Arm Assembly Parts Matrix</th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Item Number</td><td>Description</td><td>Part Number</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>Idler shaft</td><td>4066386</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Idler pulley</td><td>4001566</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Idler bearing</td><td>4016800</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Dust shield</td><td>3635105</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>Spacer ring</td><td>4001568</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>Spring</td><td>3171146</td></tr></tbody></table></div><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/08600519.png\" alt=\"08600519.png\"></figure>","tsb101519":"<h2>New General Cleaning Instruction</h2><h3>Warranty Statement</h3><p>The information in this document has no effect on present warranty coverage or repair practices, nor does it authorize TRP or Campaign actions.</p><h3>Contents</h3><p>This Service/Parts Topic is a revision to Procedure <a class=\"lnk doc\" href=\"#/doc/99-204-008\">204-008</a> for General Cleaning Instructions.</p><p>Considerable concern has been generated at both the factory and the field about engine failures that occur a short time after an in-frame repair. Analysis of returned parts indicates many of the failures are the result of dirt entering the engine during the repair. In particular, intensive investigation of failures of ISX engines rod and main bearings very soon after field repairs of the oil coolers, front gear cover, or cylinder head has shown that 30 percent of the failures are due to contamination of some main oil drillings. Investigation into the type and source of the contamination indicates it is appropriate to remind, review, and revise parts cleaning instructions for all engines.</p><p>The statement &#x27;A person can spend too much time cleaning parts but a person can <b>not</b> get them too clean&#x27; is even more relevant as customer expectations and regulatory requirements increase. These demands coupled with increased diesel engine product complexity place more pressure on the field technician in terms of time required to perform a quality repair. In a effort to reduce customer downtime, many different labor saving devices, techniques, and shortcuts are being used to meet customer expectations. However, some of the shortcuts and labor saving efforts are the very things that can cause contamination of internal engine components that lead to additional failures.</p><p>Attached is the revised General Cleaning Instructions that will be placed in all service publications where those instructions are included. Field shops are strongly encouraged to review these instructions with all technicians and to include general cleaning instructions in technician training programs and new technician orientation programs.</p><div class=\"callout note\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">i</span>Note · Примечание</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>This is a product improvement <b>not</b> subject to campaign.</p></div></div>","tsb101521":"<h2>New Oil Cooler Kit</h2><h3>Warranty Statement</h3><p>The information in this document has no effect on present warranty coverage or repair practices, nor does it authorize TRP or Campaign actions.</p><h3>Contents</h3><p>This Service/Parts Topic introduces a new Oil Cooler Kit, Part Number 4089900, which supersedes Oil Cooler Kit, Part Number 4089583.</p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>New Kit Part Number 4089900</th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Description</td><td>Part Number</td><td>Quantity</td></tr><tr><td>Capscrews 12 point (short)</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/4101867\">4101867</a></td><td>4</td></tr><tr><td>Capscrews 12 point (long)</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/4101868\">4101868</a></td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>Oil cooler core gaskets (rubber flash)</td><td>4101869</td><td>3</td></tr><tr><td>Protective cover</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/4026420\">4026420</a></td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>Protective cover</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/4058956\">4058956</a></td><td>1</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>The cooler core element, Part Number 4059460, is <b>no</b> longer included in the kit. If the element fails leak testing, a replacement can be ordered separately. Analysis has shown leaks occur at the mounting joints much more often than in the cooler core elements. Elements that do <b>not</b> leak do <b>not</b> need to be replaced.</p><p>It is important to leak test the cooler core element assembly as a unit using lubricating oil cooler test kit, Part Number 4918200, so leaks at the mounting joints can be identified. These leaks can <b>not</b> be identified by testing an individual cooler core element removed from the assembly. A cooler core element is replaced <b>only</b> if it is found to be leaking.</p><p>If <b>no</b> leaks are found and the engine serial number is in the range of 14023600 to 14050000 or 79000000 to 79012457 the gaskets and capscrews <b>only</b> are replaced.</p><p>The cooler core element is to be reused. If <b>no</b> leaks are found and the engine serial number is <b>not</b> in the above ranges, the cooler core elements are <b>not</b> to be removed. Check the capscrew torques and tighten to the specified torque.</p><p>Lubricating oil cooler leak test Procedures 007-003, Lubricating Oil Cooler and 007-007, Lubricating Oil Cooler Element have been updated to include the latest leak testing procedures. These procedures <b>must</b> be followed if an element is suspected of leaking. Cooler core elements that are <b>not</b> leaking, are <b>not</b> to be replaced.</p><div class=\"callout note\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">i</span>Note · Примечание</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>This is a product improvement, <b>not</b> subject to campaign.</p></div></div>","tsb101531":"<h2>Revision of Water Pump Procedure</h2><h3>Warranty Statement</h3><p>The information in this document has no effect on present warranty coverage or repair practices, nor does it authorize TRP or Campaign actions.</p><h3>Contents</h3><p>This Service Parts Topic revises Procedure 008-062, Water Pump. This procedure affects for Signature, ISX and QSX15 engine series. The impeller cover removal and install steps are included.</p><div class=\"callout note\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">i</span>Note · Примечание</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>This is a product improvement, <b>not</b> subject to campaign.</p></div></div>","tsb101540":"<h2>Drilled and Non-Drilled Connecting Rods</h2><h3>Warranty Statement</h3><p>The information in this document has no effect on present warranty coverage or repair practices, nor does it authorize TRP or Campaign actions.</p><h3>Contents</h3><p>This Service/Parts Topic addresses the difference between the drilled and non-drilled connecting rod applications for the ISX EGR and Non-EGR series engines.</p><div class=\"callout note\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">i</span>Note · Примечание</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>This is a product improvement, <b>not</b> subject to campaign.</p></div></div><p>This topic includes the following:</p><ul><li>Engine serial number first</li><li>Parts table</li><li>General information</li><li>Repair guidelines.</li></ul><p>The engine serial number first for the single piece pistons and drilled connecting rods is 14042852. The build date is October 18, 2002</p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>ISX Drilled Connecting Rod Parts List</th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Description</td><td>Part Number</td></tr><tr><td>ISX Drilled Connecting Rod</td><td>4059429 NX</td></tr><tr><td>ISX Drilled Connecting Rod Bearing Set of 1 Bearing Set</td><td>4089405</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>ISX Non-Drilled Connecting Rod Parts List</th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Description</td><td>Part Number</td></tr><tr><td>ISX Non-Drilled Connecting Rod</td><td>3681343 NX/RX</td></tr><tr><td>ISX Non-Drilled Connecting Rod Bearing Set of 1 Bearing Set</td><td>4089139</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Parts Supercession</th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Description</td><td>Old Part Number</td><td>New Part Number</td></tr><tr><td>ISX Non-Drilled Connecting Rod</td><td>3681343 NX/RX</td><td>4059429 NX</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>With the introduction of the single piece (Monotherm) piston, the ISX engine went through several cylinder kit component changes. Engines equipped with single piece pistons require redesigned piston pins, drilled connecting rods, drilled upper rod bearings, and updated ring sets. This piston pin joint <b>must</b> be lubricated, which is provided by the drilled connecting rod and matching drilled upper rod bearing. Single piece pistons are lighter than the two piece articulated pistons and are <b>not</b> to be mixed within the same engine. However, drilled and non-drilled connecting rods can be mixed within the same engine provided the two piece articulated piston is used. Rod bearings <b>must</b> be matched with the connecting rods; drilled connecting rods <b>must</b> use drilled rod bearings and non-drilled rods <b>must</b> use non-drilled rod bearings.</p><div class=\"callout warning\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">⚠</span>CAUTION · Осторожно</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Single piece pistons cannot be mixed with two piece pistons within the same engine. Mixing single piece with two piece pistons within the same engine will cause engine damage.</p></div></div><ul><li>Non-drilled and drilled connecting rods can be mixed in the same engine.</li><li>Rod bearings <b>must</b> match the connecting rod design.</li><li>Single piece pistons can <b>not</b> be mixed with two piece pistons within the same engine.</li></ul><ul><li>Drilled connecting rods <b>must</b> be fitted with drilled rod bearings.</li><li>Single piece pistons can <b>not</b> be mixed with two piece pistons within the same engine.</li></ul>","tsb101545":"<h2>Fuel Shutoff Valve Solenoid Resistance Specification</h2><h3>Warranty Statement</h3><p>The information in this document has no effect on present warranty coverage or repair practices, nor does it authorize TRP or Campaign actions.</p><h3>Contents</h3><p>The purpose of this Service/Parts Topic is to notify the field of a change in electrical resistance specifications for the fuel shutoff valve solenoid. The new resistance specifications are more accurate than the old specifications. The new specifications are listed in the table below.</p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Revised Fuel System Shutoff Valve Specifications</th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Voltage</td><td>Resistance Minimum (Ohms)</td><td>Resistance Maximum (Ohms)</td></tr><tr><td>6 VDC</td><td>1</td><td>5</td></tr><tr><td>12 VDC</td><td>6</td><td>15</td></tr><tr><td>24 VDC</td><td>24</td><td>50</td></tr><tr><td>32 VDC</td><td>42</td><td>80</td></tr><tr><td>36 VDC</td><td>46</td><td>87</td></tr><tr><td>48 VDC</td><td>92</td><td>145</td></tr><tr><td>74 VDC</td><td>315</td><td>375</td></tr><tr><td>115 VAC</td><td>645</td><td>735</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>In all cases the solenoid terminals <b>must</b> be thoroughly cleaned before the measurement is taken. In the case of the single pole solenoids, it is recommended to scrape paint off another portion of the solenoid cast iron housing. Good contact pressure with the multimeter terminals is recommended as well as a stable measurement on the display before the reading is compared against the specifications.</p><p>Procedure 005-043 (Fuel Shutoff Valve) is being updated to reflect the new specifications.</p>","tsb101548":"<h2>Main Bearing Capscrew Procedure Revision</h2><h3>Warranty Statement</h3><p>The information in this document has no effect on present warranty coverage or repair practices, nor does it authorize TRP or Campaign actions.</p><h3>Contents</h3><p>This Service/Parts Topic introduces a new torque specification for use in the field during the installation of main bearing capscrews on the ISX engine family. This change will affect <b>only</b> older blocks produced prior to engine serial number (ESN) 79012120. The present specification will continue to be used on newer engines.</p><p>The procedure change will affect only the torque specifications.</p><p>This topic includes the following:</p><ul><li>Engine serial numbers which are affected by this change</li><li>Instructions on determining the older USA block and the present production MEX block</li><li>The new torque procedure specifications.</li></ul><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Table 1, ISX Engine Serial Number and Build Date Range</th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Application</td><td>Engine Serial Number Range</td><td>Build date Range</td></tr><tr><td>ISX, Signature, QSX</td><td>14000000 - 79012120</td><td>6/24/98 through 6/02/03</td></tr></tbody></table></div>","tsb101553":"<h2>Fuel Shutoff Valve Solenoid Resistance Specification</h2><h3>Warranty Statement</h3><p>The information in this document has no effect on present warranty coverage or repair practices, nor does it authorize TRP or Campaign actions.</p><h3>Contents</h3><p>The purpose of this Service/Parts Topic is to notify the field of a change in electrical resistance specifications for the fuel shutoff valve solenoid. The new resistance specifications are more accurate than the old specifications. The new specifications are listed in the table below.</p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Revised Fuel System Shutoff Valve Solenoid Specifications</th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Voltage</td><td>Resistance Minimum (Ohms)</td><td>Resistance Maximum (Ohms)</td></tr><tr><td>6 VDC</td><td>1</td><td>5</td></tr><tr><td>12 VDC</td><td>6</td><td>15</td></tr><tr><td>24 VDC</td><td>24</td><td>50</td></tr><tr><td>32 VDC</td><td>42</td><td>80</td></tr><tr><td>36 VDC</td><td>46</td><td>87</td></tr><tr><td>48 VDC</td><td>92</td><td>145</td></tr><tr><td>74 VDC</td><td>315</td><td>375</td></tr><tr><td>115 VAC</td><td>645</td><td>735</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>In all cases the solenoid terminals <b>must</b> be thoroughly cleaned before the measurement is taken. In the case of the single pole solenoids, it is recommended to scrape paint off another portion of the solenoid cast iron housing. Good contact pressure with the multimeter terminals is recommended as well as a stable measurement on the display before the reading is compared against the specifications.</p><p>Procedure 005-043 (Fuel Shutoff Valve) is being updated to reflect the new specifications.</p>","tsb101557":"<h2>Fuel Shutoff Valve Solenoid Resistance Specification</h2><h3>Warranty Statement</h3><p>The information in this document has no effect on present warranty coverage or repair practices, nor does it authorize TRP or Campaign actions.</p><h3>Contents</h3><p>The purpose of this Service/Parts Topic is to notify the field of a change in electrical resistance specifications for the fuel shutoff valve solenoid. The new resistance specifications are more accurate than the old specifications. The new specifications are listed in the table below.</p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Revised Fuel System Shutoff Valve Solenoid Specifications</th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Voltage</td><td>Resistance Minimum (Ohms)</td><td>Resistance Maximum (Ohms)</td></tr><tr><td>6 VDC</td><td>1</td><td>5</td></tr><tr><td>12 VDC</td><td>6</td><td>15</td></tr><tr><td>24 VDC</td><td>24</td><td>50</td></tr><tr><td>32 VDC</td><td>42</td><td>80</td></tr><tr><td>36 VDC</td><td>46</td><td>87</td></tr><tr><td>48 VDC</td><td>92</td><td>145</td></tr><tr><td>74 VDC</td><td>315</td><td>375</td></tr><tr><td>115 VAC</td><td>645</td><td>735</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>In all cases the solenoid terminals <b>must</b> be thoroughly cleaned before the measurement is taken. In the case of the single pole solenoids, it is recommended to scrape paint off another portion of the solenoid cast iron housing. Good contact pressure with the multimeter terminals is recommended as well as a stable measurement on the display before the reading is compared against the specifications.</p><p>Procedures 005-043 (Fuel Shutoff Valve) are being updated to reflect the new specifications.</p>","tsb101558":"<h2>Fuel Shutoff Valve Solenoid Resistance Specification</h2><h3>Warranty Statement</h3><p>The information in this document has no effect on present warranty coverage or repair practices, nor does it authorize TRP or Campaign actions.</p><h3>Contents</h3><p>The purpose of this Service/Parts Topic is to notify the field of a change in electrical resistance specifications for the fuel shutoff valve solenoid. The new resistance specifications are more accurate than the old specifications. The new specifications are listed in the table below.</p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Revised Fuel System Shutoff Valve Solenoid Specifications</th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Voltage</td><td>Resistance Minimum (Ohms)</td><td>Resistance Maximum (Ohms)</td></tr><tr><td>6 VDC</td><td>1</td><td>5</td></tr><tr><td>12 VDC</td><td>6</td><td>15</td></tr><tr><td>24 VDC</td><td>24</td><td>50</td></tr><tr><td>32 VDC</td><td>42</td><td>80</td></tr><tr><td>36 VDC</td><td>46</td><td>87</td></tr><tr><td>48 VDC</td><td>92</td><td>145</td></tr><tr><td>74 VDC</td><td>315</td><td>375</td></tr><tr><td>115 VAC</td><td>645</td><td>735</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>In all cases the solenoid terminals <b>must</b> be thoroughly cleaned before the measurement is taken. In the case of the single pole solenoids, it is recommended to scrape paint off another portion of the solenoid cast iron housing. Good contact pressure with the multimeter terminals is recommended as well as a stable measurement on the display before the reading is compared against the specifications.</p><p>The Engine Performance Tree is being updated to reflect the new specifications.</p>","tsb101560":"<h2>Fuel Shutoff Valve Solenoid Resistance Specification</h2><h3>Warranty Statement</h3><p>The information in this document has no effect on present warranty coverage or repair practices, nor does it authorize TRP or Campaign actions.</p><h3>Contents</h3><p>The purpose of this Service/Parts Topic is to notify the field of a change in electrical resistance specifications for the fuel shutoff valve solenoid. The new resistance specifications are more accurate than the old specifications. The new specifications are listed in the table below.</p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Revised Fuel System Shutoff Valve Solenoid Solenoid Specifications</th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Voltage</td><td>Resistance Minimum (Ohms)</td><td>Resistance Maximum (Ohms)</td></tr><tr><td>6 VDC</td><td>1</td><td>5</td></tr><tr><td>12 VDC</td><td>6</td><td>15</td></tr><tr><td>24 VDC</td><td>24</td><td>50</td></tr><tr><td>32 VDC</td><td>42</td><td>80</td></tr><tr><td>36 VDC</td><td>46</td><td>87</td></tr><tr><td>48 VDC</td><td>92</td><td>145</td></tr><tr><td>74 VDC</td><td>315</td><td>375</td></tr><tr><td>115 VAC</td><td>645</td><td>735</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>In all cases the solenoid terminals <b>must</b> be thoroughly cleaned before the measurement is taken. In the case of the single pole solenoids, it is recommended to scrape paint off another portion of the solenoid cast iron housing. Good contact pressure with the multimeter terminals is recommended as well as a stable measurement on the display before the reading is compared against the specifications.</p><p>Procedure 019-050 (Fuel Shutoff Valve) is being updated to reflect the new specifications.</p>","tsb101562":"<h2>Fuel Shutoff Valve Solenoid Resistance Specification</h2><h3>Warranty Statement</h3><p>The information in this document has no effect on present warranty coverage or repair practices, nor does it authorize TRP or Campaign actions.</p><h3>Contents</h3><p>The purpose of this Service/Parts Topic is to notify the field of a change in electrical resistance specifications for the fuel shutoff valve solenoid. The new resistance specifications are more accurate than the old specifications. The new specifications are listed in the table below.</p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Revised Fuel System Shutoff Valve Solenoid Specifications</th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Voltage</td><td>Resistance Minimum (Ohms)</td><td>Resistance Maximum (Ohms)</td></tr><tr><td>6 VDC</td><td>1</td><td>5</td></tr><tr><td>12 VDC</td><td>6</td><td>15</td></tr><tr><td>24 VDC</td><td>24</td><td>50</td></tr><tr><td>32 VDC</td><td>42</td><td>80</td></tr><tr><td>36 VDC</td><td>46</td><td>87</td></tr><tr><td>48 VDC</td><td>92</td><td>145</td></tr><tr><td>74 VDC</td><td>315</td><td>375</td></tr><tr><td>115 VAC</td><td>645</td><td>735</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>In all cases the solenoid terminals <b>must</b> be thoroughly cleaned before the measurement is taken. In the case of the single pole solenoids, it is recommended to scrape paint off another portion of the solenoid cast iron housing. Good contact pressure with the multimeter terminals is recommended as well as a stable measurement on the display before the reading is compared against the specifications.</p><p>Procedure 019-050 (Fuel Shutoff Valve) is being updated to reflect the new specifications.</p>","tsb101572":"<h2>Fuel Shutoff Valve Solenoid Resistance Specification</h2><h3>Warranty Statement</h3><p>The information in this document has no effect on present warranty coverage or repair practices, nor does it authorize TRP or Campaign actions.</p><h3>Contents</h3><p>The purpose of this Service/Parts Topic is to notify the field of a change in electrical resistance specifications for the fuel shutoff valve solenoid. The new resistance specifications are more accurate than the old specifications. The new specifications are listed in the table below.</p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Revised Fuel System Shutoff Valve Solenoid Specifications</th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Voltage</td><td>Resistance Minimum (Ohms)</td><td>Resistance Maximum (Ohms)</td></tr><tr><td>6 VDC</td><td>1</td><td>5</td></tr><tr><td>12 VDC</td><td>6</td><td>15</td></tr><tr><td>24 VDC</td><td>24</td><td>50</td></tr><tr><td>32 VDC</td><td>42</td><td>80</td></tr><tr><td>36 VDC</td><td>46</td><td>87</td></tr><tr><td>48 VDC</td><td>92</td><td>145</td></tr><tr><td>74 VDC</td><td>315</td><td>375</td></tr><tr><td>115 VAC</td><td>645</td><td>735</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>In all cases the solenoid terminals <b>must</b> be thoroughly cleaned before the measurement is taken. In the case of the single pole solenoids, it is recommended to scrape paint off another portion of the solenoid cast iron housing. Good contact pressure with the multimeter terminals is recommended as well as a stable measurement on the display before the reading is compared against the specifications.</p><p>Fault Code 254 and Fault Code 259 are being updated to reflect the new specifications.</p>","tsb101573":"<h2>Fuel Shutoff Valve Solenoid Resistance Specification</h2><h3>Warranty Statement</h3><p>The information in this document has no effect on present warranty coverage or repair practices, nor does it authorize TRP or Campaign actions.</p><h3>Contents</h3><p>The purpose of this Service/Parts Topic is to notify the field of a change in electrical resistance specifications for the fuel shutoff valve solenoid. The new resistance specifications are more accurate than the old specifications. The new specifications are listed in the table below.</p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Revised Fuel System Shutoff Valve Solenoid Specifications</th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Voltage</td><td>Resistance Minimum (Ohms)</td><td>Resistance Maximum (Ohms)</td></tr><tr><td>6 VDC</td><td>1</td><td>5</td></tr><tr><td>12 VDC</td><td>6</td><td>15</td></tr><tr><td>24 VDC</td><td>24</td><td>50</td></tr><tr><td>32 VDC</td><td>42</td><td>80</td></tr><tr><td>36 VDC</td><td>46</td><td>87</td></tr><tr><td>48 VDC</td><td>92</td><td>145</td></tr><tr><td>74 VDC</td><td>315</td><td>375</td></tr><tr><td>115 VAC</td><td>645</td><td>735</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>In all cases the solenoid terminals <b>must</b> be thoroughly cleaned before the measurement is taken. In the case of the single pole solenoids, it is recommended to scrape paint off another portion of the solenoid cast iron housing. Good contact pressure with the multimeter terminals is recommended as well as a stable measurement on the display before the reading is compared against the specifications.</p><p>Fault Code 254 is being updated to reflect the new specifications.</p>","tsb101577":"<h2>Fuel Shutoff Valve Solenoid Resistance Specification</h2><h3>Warranty Statement</h3><p>The information in this document has no effect on present warranty coverage or repair practices, nor does it authorize TRP or Campaign actions.</p><h3>Contents</h3><p>The purpose of this Service/Parts Topic is to notify the field of a change in electrical resistance specifications for the fuel shutoff valve solenoid. The new resistance specifications are more accurate than the old specifications. The new specifications are listed in the table below.</p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Revised Fuel System Shutoff Valve Solenoid Specifications</th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Voltage</td><td>Resistance Minimum (Ohms)</td><td>Resistance Maximum (Ohms)</td></tr><tr><td>6 VDC</td><td>1</td><td>5</td></tr><tr><td>12 VDC</td><td>6</td><td>15</td></tr><tr><td>24 VDC</td><td>24</td><td>50</td></tr><tr><td>32 VDC</td><td>42</td><td>80</td></tr><tr><td>36 VDC</td><td>46</td><td>87</td></tr><tr><td>48 VDC</td><td>92</td><td>145</td></tr><tr><td>74 VDC</td><td>315</td><td>375</td></tr><tr><td>115 VAC</td><td>645</td><td>735</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>In all cases the solenoid terminals <b>must</b> be thoroughly cleaned before the measurement is taken. In the case of the single pole solenoids, it is recommended to scrape paint off another portion of the solenoid cast iron housing. Good contact pressure with the multimeter terminals is recommended as well as a stable measurement on the display before the reading is compared against the specifications.</p><p>Fault Code 254 is being updated to reflect the new specifications.</p>","tsb101579":"<h2>Plunger Lift Timing Procedure and Tool Addition</h2><h3>Warranty Statement</h3><p>The information in this document has no effect on present warranty coverage or repair practices, nor does it authorize TRP or Campaign actions.</p><h3>Contents</h3><p>This Service/Parts Topic revises Procedure 005-013, Fuel Injection Pump, In-Line, Spill Port Timing.</p><div class=\"callout note\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">i</span>Note · Примечание</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>This is a product improvement and <b>not</b> subject to campaign.</p></div></div><p>The following is a part number list of Bosch® P7100 fuel injection pumps that use delivery valve gaskets. Pumps used in ratings developed after these will use delivery valves without gaskets. If the fuel injection pump was developed in North America and is <b>not</b> on this list, it will be without delivery valve gaskets. For assistance in identifying the fuel injection pump delivery valve type contact a Cummins® Authorized Repair Facility.</p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Injection Pump Part Number</th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>3913340</td><td>3921922</td></tr><tr><td>3916626</td><td>3922424</td></tr><tr><td>3916627</td><td>3921923</td></tr><tr><td>3916628</td><td>3921925</td></tr><tr><td>3916629</td><td>3921970</td></tr><tr><td>3917088</td><td>3922425</td></tr><tr><td>3917089</td><td>3922426</td></tr><tr><td>3918321</td><td>3922427</td></tr><tr><td>3919090</td><td>3922446</td></tr><tr><td>3921769</td><td>3922449</td></tr><tr><td>3921770</td><td>3922471</td></tr><tr><td>3921771</td><td>3924903</td></tr><tr><td>3921772</td><td>3925085</td></tr><tr><td>3921773</td><td>3925086</td></tr><tr><td>3921774</td><td>3926603</td></tr><tr><td>3921775</td><td>3927923</td></tr><tr><td>3921776</td><td>3927924</td></tr><tr><td>3921777</td><td>3928169</td></tr><tr><td>3921918</td><td>3928412</td></tr><tr><td>3921920</td><td>3928606</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>The following table supplies the lift specifications for the Bosch® P7100 fuel injection pumps <b>only</b>. The allowable tolerance range for timing is ± 0.1 mm [0.004 inch].</p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th></th><th>CPLs</th><th>CPLs</th><th>CPLs</th><th>CPLs</th><th>CPLs</th><th>CPLs</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Static Injection Timing (from engine dataplate, degrees BTDC)</td><td>987, 1126, 1262, 1263, 1406, 1422, 1582, 1618, 1850, 2118, 2196</td><td>1549, 1550, 1551, 1552, 1553, 1815, 1816, 1839, 1914, 1915, 1916, 1959</td><td>1863, 1968, 2022, 2174, 2265, 2268, 2308</td><td>2023, 2175</td><td>563, 1566, 1569, 1658, 1812, 1905, 1917, 1918, 1920, 1922, 1923, 1949, 1950, 1972, 2034, 2101, 2103, 2104, 2106, 2107, 2108, 2150</td><td>1564, 1919, 2011, 2102, 2105, 2158, 2169, 2191</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>Lift Specification</td><td>Lift Specification</td><td>Lift Specification</td><td>Lift Specification</td><td>Lift Specification</td><td>Lift Specification</td></tr><tr><td>9.5</td><td>6.05 mm</td><td>5.15 mm</td><td>N/A</td><td>N/A</td><td>5.65 mm</td><td>5.6 mm</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td>6.15 mm</td><td>5.25 mm</td><td>N/A</td><td>N/A</td><td>5.75 mm</td><td>5.7 mm</td></tr><tr><td>10.5</td><td>6.25 mm</td><td>5.35 mm</td><td>N/A</td><td>N/A</td><td>5.85 mm</td><td>5.8 mm</td></tr><tr><td>11</td><td>6.35 mm</td><td>5.45 mm</td><td>4.0 mm</td><td>N/A</td><td>5.95 mm</td><td>5.9 mm</td></tr><tr><td>11.5</td><td>6.4 mm</td><td>5.55 mm</td><td>4.05 mm</td><td>4.7 mm</td><td>6.05 mm</td><td>6 mm</td></tr><tr><td>12</td><td>6.5 mm</td><td>5.65 mm</td><td>4.15 mm</td><td>4.8 mm</td><td>6.15 mm</td><td>6.05 mm</td></tr><tr><td>12.5</td><td>6.6 mm</td><td>5.7 mm</td><td>4.2 mm</td><td>4.9 mm</td><td>6.25 mm</td><td>6.15 mm</td></tr><tr><td>13</td><td>6.7 mm</td><td>5.8 mm</td><td>4.3 mm</td><td>5 mm</td><td>6.35 mm</td><td>6.25 mm</td></tr><tr><td>13.5</td><td>6.8 mm</td><td>5.9 mm</td><td>4.4 mm</td><td>5.1 mm</td><td>6.45 mm</td><td>6.35 mm</td></tr><tr><td>14</td><td>N/A</td><td>6.0 mm</td><td>4.5 mm</td><td>5.2 mm</td><td>N/A</td><td>N/A</td></tr><tr><td>14.5</td><td>N/A</td><td>6.1 mm</td><td>4.6 mm</td><td>5.3 mm</td><td>N/A</td><td>N/A</td></tr><tr><td>15</td><td>N/A</td><td>N/A</td><td>4.7 mm</td><td>5.4 mm</td><td>N/A</td><td>N/A</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>The following table supplies the lift specifications for the Marine Midrange engines. The nominal value for control parts list (CPL) 1975 is 22 degrees and CPL 2172 and 2208 is 24 degrees. The allowable tolerance range for timing remains at ± 0.1 mm [0.004 inch].</p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th></th><th>Control Parts List (CPL)</th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Static Injection</td><td>Marine B Series</td><td>Marine C Series</td></tr><tr><td>Lift Value BTDC</td><td>CPLs 1975, 2208</td><td>CPL 2172</td></tr><tr><td>20.0</td><td>7.90 mm</td><td>7.29 mm</td></tr><tr><td>20.5</td><td>8.02 mm</td><td>7.40 mm</td></tr><tr><td>21.0</td><td>8.14 mm</td><td>7.52 mm</td></tr><tr><td>21.5</td><td>8.26 mm</td><td>7.63 mm</td></tr><tr><td>22.0</td><td>8.38 mm</td><td>7.75 mm</td></tr><tr><td>22.5</td><td>8.51 mm</td><td>7.87 mm</td></tr><tr><td>23.0</td><td>8.63 mm</td><td>7.98 mm</td></tr><tr><td>23.5</td><td>8.75 mm</td><td>8.10 mm</td></tr><tr><td>24.0</td><td>8.88 mm</td><td>8.22 mm</td></tr><tr><td>24.5</td><td>9.01 mm</td><td>8.34 mm</td></tr><tr><td>25.0</td><td>9.13 mm</td><td>8.47 mm</td></tr><tr><td>25.5</td><td>9.26 mm</td><td>8.59 mm</td></tr><tr><td>26.0</td><td>9.39 mm</td><td>8.71 mm</td></tr></tbody></table></div>","tsb101583":"<h2>Fuel Shutoff Valve Solenoid Resistance Specification</h2><h3>Warranty Statement</h3><p>The information in this document has no effect on present warranty coverage or repair practices, nor does it authorize TRP or Campaign actions.</p><h3>Contents</h3><p>The purpose of this Service/Parts Topic is to notify the field of a change in electrical resistance specifications for the fuel shutoff valve solenoid. The new resistance specifications are more accurate than the old specifications. The new specifications are listed in the table below.</p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Revised Fuel System Shutoff Valve Solenoid Specifications</th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Voltage</td><td>Resistance Minimum (Ohms)</td><td>Resistance Maximum (Ohms)</td></tr><tr><td>6 VDC</td><td>1</td><td>5</td></tr><tr><td>12 VDC</td><td>6</td><td>15</td></tr><tr><td>24 VDC</td><td>24</td><td>50</td></tr><tr><td>32 VDC</td><td>42</td><td>80</td></tr><tr><td>36 VDC</td><td>46</td><td>87</td></tr><tr><td>48 VDC</td><td>92</td><td>145</td></tr><tr><td>74 VDC</td><td>315</td><td>375</td></tr><tr><td>115 VAC</td><td>645</td><td>735</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>In all cases the solenoid terminals <b>must</b> be thoroughly cleaned before the measurement is taken. In the case of the single pole solenoids, it is recommended to scrape paint off another portion of the solenoid cast iron housing. Good contact pressure with the multimeter terminals is recommended as well as a stable measurement on the display before the reading is compared against the specifications.</p><p>The Engine Performance Tree is being updated to reflect the new specifications.</p>","tsb101586":"<h2>New Oil Suction Tube Bracket</h2><h3>Warranty Statement</h3><p>The information in this document has no effect on present warranty coverage or repair practices, nor does it authorize TRP or Campaign actions.</p><h3>Contents</h3><p>This Service/Parts topic introduces a new oil suction tube bracket for the QST30 Industrial engines.</p><p>The existing bracket is a singular column of angled iron shown in Figure 1 and will continue to be used on Power Generation engines. The new Industrial bracket is triangular in shape and is shown in Figure 2.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/07a00141.png\" alt=\"07a00141.png\"></figure><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/07a00142.png\" alt=\"07a00142.png\"></figure><p>Figure 1, Existing Singular Column Bracket</p><p>Figure 2, New Triangular Shaped Bracket</p><p>The repair procedures will <b>not</b> change.</p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Existing Part Number</th><th>New Part Number</th><th>Quantity</th><th>Description</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>3094397</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/4068734\">4068734</a></td><td>1</td><td>Bracket, oil tube</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"callout note\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">i</span>Note · Примечание</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>This is a product improvement and is <b>not</b> subject to campaign.</p></div></div>","tsb101589":"<h2>Revision to Lubricating Oil Suction Tube (Block Mounted)</h2><h3>Warranty Statement</h3><p>The information in this document has no effect on present warranty coverage or repair practices, nor does it authorize TRP or Campaign actions.</p><h3>Contents</h3><p>This Service/Parts Topic revises Procedure 007-035, Lubricating Oil Suction Tube (Block Mounted), updating the capscrew torque information.</p><div class=\"callout note\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">i</span>Note · Примечание</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>This is a product improvement and <b>not</b> subject to campaign.</p></div></div>","tsb101591":"<h2>Crankshaft Inspection</h2><h3>Warranty Statement</h3><p>The information in this document has no effect on present warranty coverage or repair practices, nor does it authorize TRP or Campaign actions.</p><h3>Contents</h3><p>This Service/Parts Topic introduces a new procedure for inspecting the ISX series engine crankshaft.</p>","tsb101596":"<h2>Cylinder Head and Main Bearing Capscrew Thread Repair</h2><h3>Warranty Statement</h3><p>The information in this document has no effect on present warranty coverage or repair practices, nor does it authorize TRP or Campaign actions.</p><h3>Contents</h3><p>This document introduces the required components to salvage damaged cylinder head and main bearing capscrew hole threads on the ISX and QSX15 series engines.</p><p>The illustration and table below show the items required in addition to the thread salvage kit, Part Number 3164021.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/22a00124.png\" alt=\"22a00124.png\"></figure><p>Figure 1, Items Purchased Separately.</p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Table 1: Items Use to Salvage ISX Main Bearing and Cylinder Head Capscrew Hole Threads</th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Item Number</td><td>Part Number</td><td>Description</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>3163842</td><td>Jam nut</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>3164445</td><td>Thread insert - 50 mm [2 in]</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>3163717</td><td>Thread insert - 76 mm [3 in]</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>3163899</td><td>Reamer</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>3163901</td><td>Tap</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>3164335</td><td>Drill</td></tr></tbody></table></div><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/22a00081.png\" alt=\"22a00081.png\"></figure><p>Figure 2, Thread Salvage Kit, Part Number 3164021.</p><ol><li>Mounting plate</li><li>Drilling fixture</li><li>Hexagon head capscrews</li><li>Heavy washer</li><li>Universal drive socket</li><li>Chip vacuum</li><li>Thread-locking compound</li><li>Primer</li><li>Locating pin</li><li>Locating pin</li><li>Stop collar</li><li>Stop collar</li><li>Spacer</li><li>Spacer</li><li>Spacer</li></ol><div class=\"callout note\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">i</span>Note · Примечание</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Included in the thread salvage kit, but <b>not</b> shown, are plain washers and a storage case.</p></div></div><p>For Related Procedures reference to the following procedure. <a class=\"lnk doc\" href=\"#/doc/10-001-026-tr\">Refer to Procedure 001-026 in Section 1.</a></p><h3>Document History</h3>","tsb101602":"<h2>Oil Cooler Specification Change</h2><h3>Warranty Statement</h3><p>The information in this document has no effect on present warranty coverage or repair practices, nor does it authorize TRP or Campaign actions.</p><h3>Contents</h3><p>This Service/Parts Topic revises Procedure 007-003 Oil Cooler Cover. Torque information discribing the installation of the 8 mm dimpled oil cooler cover is included.</p><div class=\"callout note\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">i</span>Note · Примечание</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>This is a product improvement and <b>not</b> subject to campaign.</p></div></div>","tsb101603":"<h2>New Running Level Dipstick</h2><h3>Warranty Statement</h3><p>The information in this document has no effect on present warranty coverage or repair practices, nor does it authorize TRP or Campaign actions.</p><h3>Contents</h3><p>This Service/Parts Topic announces the introduction of a new running level dipstick and hand hole cover for QSK45/60 Industrial engines. This running level dipstick has markings on both sides; one side has the normal static markings and is marked &#x27;Engine Stopped&#x27;, the other side is for the running level checks and is marked &#x27;Engine Idling&#x27;.</p><p>Service Parts can be purchased for the QSK45/60 Industrial engines. The installation and calibration procedures are detailed below.</p><p>The hand hole cover and dipstick is designed as a direct replacement for the hand hole cover containing the dipstick tube currently fitted to the engine.</p><p>Please note that depending on the harness type (phase 1.0 &amp; 1.5 versus phase 2.0), a different hand hole cover is used. All Phase 2 QSK45/60 engines will have run level hardware.</p><p>This change went into production at the Daventry Engine Plant on 3rd January 2003. The engine serial number first is 33153219.</p><div class=\"callout note\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">i</span>Note · Примечание</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>This is a product improvement <b>not</b> subject to campaign.</p></div></div>","tsb101611":"<h2>Cylinder Head Procedure Revision</h2><h3>Warranty Statement</h3><p>The information in this document has no effect on present warranty coverage or repair practices, nor does it authorize TRP or Campaign actions.</p><h3>Contents</h3><p>This Service/Parts Topic introduces a new Protective Plug Kit for all ISX Series engines. The plugs are to be used any time a lubrication port or drilling is exposed during a repair.</p><p>If a repair exposes a filtered oil passage, it is very important to seal the oil passage drillings with the recommended plugs. Failure to insert the oil passage plugs can result in main bearing and connecting rod bearing failures. The use of power tools combined with abrasive disc pads to clean gasket surfaces can also introduce abrasive pad residue into oil passage areas. Therefore, it is <b>not</b> recommended to use power tools when cleaning gasket surfaces that have intersecting oil passages. Clean the gasket sealing surfaces by hand, with a gasket scraper or cleaning pad, Part Number 3823258. If the residual gasket material can <b>not</b> be felt with a finger, the surface is ready to accept a new gasket.</p><p>The Protective Plug Kit part numbers and descriptions are shown in the table below.</p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Table 1, Protective Plug Kit Content</th><th></th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Kit Part Number 4918319</td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Part Number</td><td>Quantity</td><td>Part Description</td><td>Color</td></tr><tr><td>4918320</td><td>1</td><td>Front block oil passage rubber plug</td><td>Black</td></tr><tr><td>4918321</td><td>1</td><td>Rear block oil passage rubber plug</td><td>Black</td></tr><tr><td>4918322</td><td>1</td><td>Oil pump to oil cooler passage rubber plug (block side)</td><td>Black</td></tr><tr><td>4918323</td><td>1</td><td>Oil cooler housing oil passage rubber plug</td><td>White</td></tr><tr><td>4918340</td><td>1</td><td>Oil cooler oil passage rubber plug (block side)</td><td>Blue</td></tr><tr><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/4089255\">4089255</a></td><td>8</td><td>Block head deck and head oil passage rubber plugs</td><td>White</td></tr></tbody></table></div>","tsb101613":"<h2>Lubricating Oil Cooler Procedure Revision</h2><h3>Warranty Statement</h3><p>The information in this document has no effect on present warranty coverage or repair practices, nor does it authorize TRP or Campaign actions.</p><h3>Contents</h3><p>This Service/Parts Topic introduces a new Protective Plug Kit for all ISX Series engines. The plugs are to be used any time a lubrication port or drilling is exposed during a repair.</p><p>If a repair exposes a filtered oil passage, it is very important to seal the oil passage drillings with the recommended plugs. Failure to insert the oil passage plugs can result in main bearing and connecting rod bearing failures. The use of power tools combined with abrasive disc pads to clean gasket surfaces can also introduce abrasive pad residue into oil passage areas. Therefore, it is <b>not</b> recommended to use power tools when cleaning gasket surfaces that have intersecting oil passages. Clean the gasket sealing surfaces by hand, with a gasket scraper or cleaning pad, Part Number 3823258. If the residual gasket material can <b>not</b> be felt with a finger, the surface is ready to accept a new gasket.</p><p>The Protective Plug Kit part numbers and descriptions are shown in the table below.</p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Table 1, Protective Plug Kit Content</th><th></th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Kit Part Number 4918319</td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Part Number</td><td>Quantity</td><td>Part Description</td><td>Color</td></tr><tr><td>4918320</td><td>1</td><td>Front block oil passage rubber plug</td><td>Black</td></tr><tr><td>4918321</td><td>1</td><td>Rear block oil passage rubber plug</td><td>Black</td></tr><tr><td>4918322</td><td>1</td><td>Oil pump to oil cooler passage rubber plug (block side)</td><td>Black</td></tr><tr><td>4918323</td><td>1</td><td>Oil cooler housing oil passage rubber plug</td><td>White</td></tr><tr><td>4918340</td><td>1</td><td>Oil cooler oil passage rubber plug (block side)</td><td>Blue</td></tr><tr><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/4089255\">4089255</a></td><td>8</td><td>Block head deck and head oil passage rubber plugs</td><td>White</td></tr></tbody></table></div>","tsb101614":"<h2>Front Gear Housing Procedure Revision</h2><h3>Warranty Statement</h3><p>The information in this document has no effect on present warranty coverage or repair practices, nor does it authorize TRP or Campaign actions.</p><h3>Contents</h3><p>This Service/Parts Topic introduces a new Protective Plug Kit for all ISX Series engines. The plugs are to be used any time a lubrication port or drilling is exposed during a repair.</p><p>If a repair exposes a filtered oil passage, it is very important to seal the oil passage drillings with the recommended plugs. Failure to insert the oil passage plugs can result in main bearing and connecting rod bearing failures. The use of power tools combined with abrasive disc pads to clean gasket surfaces can also introduce abrasive pad residue into oil passage areas. Therefore, it is <b>not</b> recommended to use power tools when cleaning gasket surfaces that have intersecting oil passages. Clean the gasket sealing surfaces by hand, with a gasket scraper or cleaning pad, Part Number 3823258. If the residual gasket material can <b>not</b> be felt with a finger, the surface is ready to accept a new gasket.</p><p>The Protective Plug Kit part numbers and descriptions are shown in the table below.</p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Table 1, Protective Plug Kit Content</th><th></th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Kit Part Number 4918319</td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Part Number</td><td>Quantity</td><td>Part Description</td><td>Color</td></tr><tr><td>4918320</td><td>1</td><td>Front block oil passage rubber plug</td><td>Black</td></tr><tr><td>4918321</td><td>1</td><td>Rear block oil passage rubber plug</td><td>Black</td></tr><tr><td>4918322</td><td>1</td><td>Oil pump to oil cooler passage rubber plug (block side)</td><td>Black</td></tr><tr><td>4918323</td><td>1</td><td>Oil cooler housing oil passage rubber plug</td><td>White</td></tr><tr><td>4918340</td><td>1</td><td>Oil cooler oil passage rubber plug (block side)</td><td>Blue</td></tr><tr><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/4089255\">4089255</a></td><td>8</td><td>Block head deck and head oil passage rubber plugs</td><td>White</td></tr></tbody></table></div>","tsb101615":"<h2>Flywheel Housing Procedure Revisions</h2><h3>Warranty Statement</h3><p>The information in this document has no effect on present warranty coverage or repair practices, nor does it authorize TRP or Campaign actions.</p><h3>Contents</h3><p>This Service/Parts Topic introduces a new Protective Plug Kit for all ISX Series engines. The plugs are to be used any time a lubrication port or drilling is exposed during a repair.</p><p>If a repair exposes a filtered oil passage, it is very important to seal the oil passage drillings with the recommended plugs. Failure to insert the oil passage plugs can result in main bearing and connecting rod bearing failures. The use of power tools combined with abrasive disc pads to clean gasket surfaces can also introduce abrasive pad residue into oil passage areas. Therefore, it is <b>not</b> recommended to use power tools when cleaning gasket surfaces that have intersecting oil passages. Clean the gasket sealing surfaces by hand, with a gasket scraper or cleaning pad, Part Number 3823258. If the residual gasket material can <b>not</b> be felt with a finger, the surface is ready to accept a new gasket.</p><p>The Protective Plug Kit part numbers and descriptions are shown in the table below.</p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Table 1, Protective Plug Kit Content</th><th></th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Kit Part Number 4918319</td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Part Number</td><td>Quantity</td><td>Part Description</td><td>Color</td></tr><tr><td>4918320</td><td>1</td><td>Front block oil passage rubber plug</td><td>Black</td></tr><tr><td>4918321</td><td>1</td><td>Rear block oil passage rubber plug</td><td>Black</td></tr><tr><td>4918322</td><td>1</td><td>Oil pump to oil cooler passage rubber plug (block side)</td><td>Black</td></tr><tr><td>4918323</td><td>1</td><td>Oil cooler housing oil passage rubber plug</td><td>White</td></tr><tr><td>4918340</td><td>1</td><td>Oil cooler oil passage rubber plug (block side)</td><td>Blue</td></tr><tr><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/4089255\">4089255</a></td><td>8</td><td>Block head deck and head oil passage rubber plugs</td><td>White</td></tr></tbody></table></div>","tsb101616":"<h2>Service Tool Procedure Revisions</h2><h3>Warranty Statement</h3><p>The information in this document has no effect on present warranty coverage or repair practices, nor does it authorize TRP or Campaign actions.</p><h3>Contents</h3><p>This Service/Parts Topic introduces a new Protective Plug Kit for all ISX Series engines. The plugs are to be used any time a lubrication port or drilling is exposed during a repair.</p><p>If a repair exposes a filtered oil passage, it is very important to seal the oil passage drillings with the recommended plugs. Failure to insert the oil passage plugs can result in main bearing and connecting rod bearing failures. The use of power tools combined with abrasive disc pads to clean gasket surfaces can also introduce abrasive pad residue into oil passage areas. Therefore, it is <b>not</b> recommended to use power tools when cleaning gasket surfaces that have intersecting oil passages. Clean the gasket sealing surfaces by hand, with a gasket scraper or cleaning pad, Part Number 3823258. If the residual gasket material can <b>not</b> be felt with a finger, the surface is ready to accept a new gasket.</p><p>The Protective Plug Kit part numbers and descriptions are shown in the table below.</p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Table 1, Protective Plug Kit Content</th><th></th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Kit Part Number 4918319</td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Part Number</td><td>Quantity</td><td>Part Description</td><td>Color</td></tr><tr><td>4918320</td><td>1</td><td>Front block oil passage rubber plug</td><td>Black</td></tr><tr><td>4918321</td><td>1</td><td>Rear block oil passage rubber plug</td><td>Black</td></tr><tr><td>4918322</td><td>1</td><td>Oil pump to oil cooler passage rubber plug (block side)</td><td>Black</td></tr><tr><td>4918323</td><td>1</td><td>Oil cooler housing oil passage rubber plug</td><td>White</td></tr><tr><td>4918340</td><td>1</td><td>Oil cooler oil passage rubber plug (block side)</td><td>Blue</td></tr><tr><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/4089255\">4089255</a></td><td>8</td><td>Block head deck and head oil passage rubber plugs</td><td>White</td></tr></tbody></table></div>","tsb101624":"<h2>Oil Pan Mounting Capscrew Torque Change</h2><h3>Warranty Statement</h3><p>The information in this document has no effect on present warranty coverage or repair practices, nor does it authorize TRP or Campaign actions.</p><h3>Contents</h3><p>This Service/Parts Topic updates the oil pan mounting capscrew torque specification in Procedure 007-025 (Lubricating Oil Pan). This change updates the service torque specification to the value currently being used in the factory. The new torque specification is to be used for <b>all</b> field repairs.</p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>New Torque Specification</th><th>Old Torque Specification</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>54 N•m [40 ft-lb]</td><td>47 N•m [35 ft-lb]</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"callout note\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">i</span>Note · Примечание</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>This is a product improvement and <b>not</b> subject to campaign.</p></div></div>","tsb101631":"<h2>New Common Main Bearing Procedure</h2><h3>Warranty Statement</h3><p>The information in this document has no effect on present warranty coverage or repair practices, nor does it authorize TRP or Campaign actions.</p><h3>Contents</h3><p>This Service/Parts Topic releases a new common Main Bearing Procedure for the M11, ISM, ISM e, and QSM11 engines and revises the main bearing capscrew torque values for all torque-to-yield blocks, Engine Serial Number (ESN) first 35011095.</p>","tsb101633":"<h2>Air Compressor Installation Procedure</h2><h3>Warranty Statement</h3><p>The information in this document has no effect on present warranty coverage or repair practices, nor does it authorize TRP or Campaign actions.</p><h3>Contents</h3><p>This Service/Parts Topic updates the procedure for installing the air compressor on Signature, ISX, and QSX15 engines.</p><p>When installing an air compressor, Loctite™ 242, Part Number 3824040 or equivalent, <b>must</b> be used on capscrews which mount the air compressor to the gear housing. This seals the threads in the housing.</p><p>To evenly distribute the sealant as the capscrew is torqued, a line of sealant should be placed along the length of the capscrew.</p><div class=\"callout note\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">i</span>Note · Примечание</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>This is a product improvement and <b>not</b> subject to campaign.</p></div></div>","tsb101636":"<h2>Lubricating Oil Filter Head Adapter</h2><h3>Warranty Statement</h3><p>The information in this document has no effect on present warranty coverage or repair practices, nor does it authorize TRP or Campaign actions.</p><h3>Contents</h3><p>This Service/Parts Topic updates the removal of the oil filter head adapter in the event of a loose or missing staking pin in Procedure 007-018 (Lubricating Oil Filter Head Adapter) in the Troubleshooting and Repair Manual Signature, ISX, and QSX15 Engines, Bulletin <a class=\"lnk man\" href=\"#/manual/3666239\">3666239</a>.</p><div class=\"callout note\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">i</span>Note · Примечание</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>This is a product improvement and <b>not</b> subject to campaign.</p></div></div>","tsb101640":"<h2>Revision to Fuel Injection Pump, In-Line</h2><h3>Warranty Statement</h3><p>The information in this document has no effect on present warranty coverage or repair practices, nor does it authorize TRP or Campaign actions.</p><h3>Contents</h3><p>This Service/Parts Topic provides information on the revision of Procedure <a class=\"lnk doc\" href=\"#/doc/41-005-012\">005-012</a>, Fuel Injection Pump, In-Line for the 6C and C8.3 series engines. The fuel pump gear puller service tool has been revised.</p><div class=\"callout note\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">i</span>Note · Примечание</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>This is a product improvement and <b>not</b> subject to campaign.</p></div></div>","tsb101647":"<h2>Cummins/Fleetguard® Filter Specifications Update</h2><h3>Warranty Statement</h3><p>The information in this document has no effect on present warranty coverage or repair practices, nor does it authorize TRP or Campaign actions.</p><h3>Contents</h3><p>This Service/Parts Topic updates the Cummins/Fleetguard® Filter Specifications procedure to include the no water separation fuel prefilter.</p><div class=\"callout note\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">i</span>Note · Примечание</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>This is a product improvement and <b>not</b> subject to campaign.</p></div></div>","tsb101652":"<h2>Capscrew Torque Revision to Cylinder Head Procedure</h2><h3>Warranty Statement</h3><p>The information in this document has no effect on present warranty coverage or repair practices, nor does it authorize TRP or Campaign actions.</p><h3>Contents</h3><p>This Service/Parts Topic introduces a new long cylinder hex head flange capscrew, Part Number <a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/4923187\">4923187</a>, that supersedes Part Number 3045850. This change has been implemented as a product improvement to address capscrew corrosion failures. The Engine Serial Number first is 35124397.</p><div class=\"callout note\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">i</span>Note · Примечание</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>This is a product improvement and <b>not</b> subject to campaign.</p></div></div><p>This topic includes the following:</p><ul><li>New and old part numbers (table)</li><li>Change in torque procedure in the Install step.</li></ul><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Table - New and Old Part Numbers</th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Part Description</td><td>New Part Number</td><td>Old Part Number</td></tr><tr><td>Hex Flange Capscrew</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/4923187\">4923187</a></td><td>3045850</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>The new capscrews can be mixed with the old capscews.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/02200069.png\" alt=\"02200069.png\"></figure><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/kn8csgb.png\" alt=\"kn8csgb.png\"></figure><p>New capscrew <a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/4923187\">4923187</a></p><p>Old capscrew 3045850</p><p>The new capscrews have a grey coating on them and do <b>not</b> have the 90 degree marking on the head of the capscrew.</p>"};
